/**
 * Written profiles of what living in each London area is actually like.
 *
 * This is the part of a neighbourhood page that is not derived from the
 * dataset. Everything else on the page — rents, commute tables, lifestyle
 * scores, council tax — is computed, and is shared in shape with the
 * other 94 pages. Without this the pages are one template with the names
 * swapped, which is what AdSense rejected three times.
 *
 * Rules, the same as lib/data/guides.ts:
 *  - Write each one. No two profiles share a skeleton or an opening move.
 *  - Durable character only: housing stock, streets, parks, how the
 *    transport really behaves, who lives there, what gets missed. Nothing
 *    that goes stale in a year (named restaurants, prices, crime figures).
 *  - Never contradict the dataset. Rents, zones and scores live elsewhere
 *    and are not repeated here.
 *  - 250–350 words, two to four paragraphs. A test holds the floor and
 *    fails if two profiles share an eight-word run.
 *
 * Written in batches by borough, each checked by a person who knows the
 * area before it ships.
 */
export const NEIGHBOURHOOD_PROFILES: Record<string, string[]> = {
  // ── Lambeth ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  brixton: [
    "Brixton is the southern end of the Victoria line, and that single fact shapes daily life more than any other. Heading north in the morning you board an empty train and usually get a seat, which almost nobody else in Zone 2 can say; you are at Oxford Circus in around a quarter of an hour and King's Cross not long after. Southeastern trains from the overground station add a second route into Victoria.",
    "The centre is loud, crowded and genuinely mixed. Brixton Village and Market Row, the covered arcades off Coldharbour Lane, hold the food that made the area's reputation, and Electric Avenue and the street market still sell yams, fish and phone cases side by side. The Caribbean community that arrived with the Windrush generation remains central to the area's identity — the Black Cultural Archives sit on Windrush Square — even as rents have pushed many long-standing residents further out.",
    "Housing splits sharply. Victorian terraces on the streets towards Brockwell Park and up Brixton Hill are mostly converted into flats and house shares; large post-war estates sit alongside them. New-build blocks near the station are the priciest rentals.",
    "The trade-off is noise and intensity. Friday and Saturday nights spill onto the streets around the Academy and the bars on Atlantic Road, and the Night Tube runs through. If you want late nights on your doorstep that is the point; if you want quiet, look at the streets south of Brockwell Park instead, which are ten minutes' walk and a different mood.",
  ],

  clapham: [
    "Clapham is organised around the Common, a large, flat, open space criss-crossed by paths, with ponds, sports pitches and a bandstand. On a warm weekend it fills with picnics, five-a-side and running clubs, and that outdoor sociability is the area's whole appeal. The people who move here tend to be in their twenties and early thirties, often sharing, often in their first or second London job.",
    "The area straddles the Lambeth–Wandsworth border, and the name is used loosely. Clapham Junction, the busy rail interchange, is actually in Battersea a mile to the west; Clapham High Street, Clapham Old Town and the streets around Clapham North are the Lambeth side. Housing is overwhelmingly Victorian terraces and mansion blocks, split into flats and house shares, with larger family houses in the roads off the Common.",
    "Transport is good but crowded. The Northern line stations at Clapham North, Common and South are narrow and crowded, and at rush hour trains towards the City can arrive already full. Clapham High Street on the Overground and the Junction's mainline services give real alternatives, and many residents walk or cycle to Clapham Junction for faster trains to Waterloo and Victoria.",
    "Nightlife centres on the High Street and is loud, predictable and busy from Thursday onwards. People who stay past thirty often drift towards the quieter roads nearer Clapham South or Abbeville Village, or across to Balham, rather than leaving the area entirely. The Common stays the constant: it is where the neighbourhood actually happens, in every season.",
  ],

  stockwell: [
    "Stockwell is mostly passed through rather than talked about, which is exactly why it is worth a look. The station is an interchange for both the Victoria and Northern lines, so from one stop you can be in the West End, at King's Cross or in the City without changing, and Brixton, Clapham and Vauxhall are each a short walk or a single stop away.",
    "The area has one of London's largest Portuguese-speaking communities, concentrated along South Lambeth Road towards Vauxhall, and the cafés, bakeries and grocers there are its most distinctive feature. Away from the main roads, the Stockwell Park conservation area holds handsome early-Victorian villas and squares that surprise people who only know the traffic junction at the station. Vincent van Gogh lodged on Hackford Road as a young man, and there are quiet streets like it all around.",
    "Housing is a real mix: those conservation-area houses, mostly split into flats; Victorian terraces; and several large council estates, which make up a big share of the homes. That mix is why rents here often undercut Clapham and Kennington for similar travel times.",
    "The trade-off is that Stockwell has no real centre. There is no high street to speak of, few restaurants that draw people in, and the big roads that meet at the station — Clapham Road, Stockwell Road, South Lambeth Road — are noisy and heavy with buses. Most residents treat it as a well-connected base and go elsewhere to eat and drink, and the few who stay for years usually do so for the conservation-area streets, where a quiet square can sit two minutes from the station crowds.",
  ],

  kennington: [
    "Kennington is central London that behaves like a residential neighbourhood. Westminster and the South Bank are a walk away, yet the streets behind Kennington Road are Georgian and early-Victorian terraces with little through traffic, and Cleaver Square, with its single pub and gravel pétanque ground, feels a long way from the river.",
    "The Oval cricket ground dominates the south end of the area, and on match days the streets around Oval station fill with spectators; otherwise it is a quiet presence. Kennington Park provides the main green space, with a café, sports courts and a flower garden, and the Imperial War Museum sits in its own park on the northern edge, towards Lambeth North.",
    "On the Northern line, Kennington is where the Charing Cross and Bank branches meet and where the extension to Nine Elms and Battersea Power Station begins. That gives residents a choice of routes into the West End or the City, though it also means the station is a place people change trains, and the platforms can be busy. Buses up Kennington Road reach Waterloo in minutes.",
    "The housing stock is split between those period terraces, many of them listed and expensive, and substantial council estates, which is why the area feels mixed rather than exclusive. The practical limitations are shops and food: there is no single high street, so most weekly shopping means Elephant and Castle, Vauxhall or an online order. People move here for the location and the quiet streets, not for things to do on the doorstep.",
  ],

  vauxhall: [
    "Vauxhall is the part of Lambeth that changed fastest. The riverside between Vauxhall and Battersea Power Station, known as Nine Elms, has filled with glass residential towers over the past fifteen years, alongside the US Embassy and the long-established New Covent Garden Market. Much of the rental stock here is in those blocks: concierge desks, gyms and river views, at prices to match.",
    "Behind the towers is an older, scruffier Vauxhall. The road junction at Vauxhall Cross, one of south London's busiest bus interchanges, is loud and slow to cross on foot however it is rearranged, and the railway viaducts carry trains into Waterloo right through the middle of the area. The arches beneath them hold clubs, gyms and workshops.",
    "Vauxhall has been one of the centres of London's LGBTQ+ nightlife for decades. The Royal Vauxhall Tavern, a listed Victorian pub, is its best-known venue, and several late-licence clubs operate under the arches. On weekend nights the area is busy until the morning; during the week it can feel empty once the office workers leave.",
    "Transport is excellent. The Victoria line reaches Victoria and Oxford Circus in a few stops, South Western Railway trains are two minutes from Waterloo, and buses fan out from Vauxhall Cross across south London. Cycling along the river is straightforward. Green space is limited to Vauxhall Pleasure Gardens, Spring Gardens and the small city farm; for anything larger, Kennington Park and Battersea Park are both within reach. Vauxhall suits people who want to be close to Westminster and the river and who value a modern flat over a neighbourhood feel.",
  ],

  "herne-hill": [
    "Herne Hill sits on the Lambeth–Southwark border, where Brixton starts to give way to Dulwich, and it has the feel of a small town inside Zone 2. The centre is a short parade of shops, cafés and pubs around the railway bridge, with a Sunday market on Railton Road that draws people from well beyond the area.",
    "Brockwell Park is the reason most people choose it. The park is large, hilly and well used, with views towards the City from the top, walled gardens, a miniature railway, and Brockwell Lido, a 1930s open-air pool that is busy from early spring. The Herne Hill Velodrome, one of the oldest cycle tracks in the world and a venue at the 1948 Olympics, is just to the south-east. Dulwich Park and the woods beyond are close.",
    "The housing is mainly Victorian and Edwardian terraces, many still whole houses rather than conversions, which is why the area leans towards couples and families and why the rental market is relatively small. Flats that do come up tend to be in converted houses or small purpose-built blocks.",
    "There is no tube. Herne Hill station has Southeastern trains to Victoria and Thameslink services through Blackfriars and the City, both quick but less frequent than a tube line, especially in the evening. Many residents walk or cycle fifteen minutes to Brixton for the Victoria line. Nightlife is limited to pubs and a few bars, which suits most of the people who live here: Brixton's late nights are close enough to reach and far enough to avoid.",
  ],

  streatham: [
    "Streatham stretches along one very long road. Streatham High Road runs for well over a mile from Streatham Hill down to Streatham Common, and it is often described as one of the longest high streets in the country. It is practical rather than pretty: supermarkets, independent restaurants from a wide range of cuisines, chain shops, and a steadily growing number of bars and cafés, especially around Streatham Hill.",
    "Housing is the main attraction. Edwardian terraces, large interwar mansion blocks and 1930s flats line the side streets, and a one-bed here usually costs noticeably less than in Clapham, Brixton or Balham. Pullman Court on Streatham Hill, a listed modernist block by Frederick Gibberd, is the best-known example of the area's interwar flats, and there are many plainer relatives of it.",
    "Green space is a real strength. Streatham Common rises steeply to the Rookery, a formal garden near the mineral wells that briefly made Streatham a fashionable spa in the eighteenth century, and Tooting Bec Common borders the western side. Streatham also has an ice rink and leisure centre, which is rare in London.",
    "Transport is the catch. There is no tube, and the three stations — Streatham Hill, Streatham and Streatham Common — are served by Southern and Thameslink trains to Victoria, London Bridge and the City, which are fine at peak times and thinner in the evening. Many people take a bus to Brixton for the Victoria line, which adds time and uncertainty. Streatham suits people who want more space for their rent and who can work around a rail timetable.",
  ],

  // ── Southwark ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  peckham: [
    "Peckham's spine is Rye Lane, a long, crowded high street of hair salons, butchers, fabric shops, churches and grocers that reflects one of the largest West African communities in Britain, alongside long-established Caribbean families. It is busy from early morning, and it is the thing newcomers either love immediately or take a few weeks to adjust to.",
    "Layered on top of that is the creative Peckham that made the area's recent reputation. Copeland Park and the Bussey Building fill old industrial space with studios, clubs and events; the multi-storey car park off Rye Lane hosts Peckham Levels inside and, in summer, a rooftop bar with one of the best free views of the London skyline. Peckham Library, with its tilted green facade, won the Stirling Prize and is still a working library.",
    "The housing divides clearly. South of the station, the streets around Bellenden Road and towards Peckham Rye are Victorian terraces, many now shared flats or family houses with prices well above what Peckham once cost. North of the high street, large estates — much of them rebuilt since the 1990s — house most of the area's social tenants. Burgess Park to the north-west and Peckham Rye Common and Park to the south, with a formal Japanese-style garden, give good access to green space on both sides.",
    "There is no tube, and it matters less than you might think. Peckham Rye station has Overground trains north through Shoreditch to Dalston and Highbury, and mainline services to Victoria, London Bridge and Blackfriars. The late-night picture is less rosy: buses carry most people home, and the area around Rye Lane feels very different at 2am from how it does at noon.",
  ],

  bermondsey: [
    "Bermondsey was industrial riverside for most of its history — tanneries, wharves, biscuit and jam factories — and the buildings survived that trade. Shad Thames, east of Tower Bridge, is a canyon of warehouses joined by iron walkways, now flats; further in, former leather works line Bermondsey Street, which has become a strip of restaurants, galleries and the Fashion and Textile Museum.",
    "Weekends revolve around the railway arches. Maltby Street's small food market runs under the viaduct, and the arches heading south-east from Tower Bridge Road hold enough small breweries and taprooms that the stretch is known as the Bermondsey Beer Mile. On a Saturday afternoon it is busy with groups walking from one to the next; on a Tuesday it is a quiet industrial street.",
    "The location is the practical draw. London Bridge and the City are a walk of fifteen to twenty minutes, Tower Bridge is closer, and Bermondsey station on the Jubilee line reaches Canary Wharf in a couple of stops and Westminster and Bond Street in the other direction. Cycling into the City along quiet backstreets is easy.",
    "The rental market is a mix of loft-style warehouse conversions, which command a premium, Victorian terraces, new-build blocks, and large council estates, which still house a big share of long-standing Bermondsey families. Green space inside the neighbourhood is thin — a handful of small parks and churchyards — so most residents head east to Southwark Park or along the Thames Path. It suits people who work in the City or at London Bridge and would rather walk than commute.",
  ],

  borough: [
    "Borough is about as central as residential south London gets. The Shard, Guy's Hospital and London Bridge station sit at its northern edge, Tate Modern and the South Bank are ten minutes' walk west, and the City is across the river. Living here means walking to work, or to almost anything, is the default.",
    "Borough Market is the landmark, and it shapes the rhythm of the week. Its produce stalls and food traders draw huge crowds from Thursday to Saturday, as do the pubs around it and along Borough High Street; Southwark Cathedral sits quietly beside it. Early on a Sunday or late on a weekday evening, the same streets can be almost empty.",
    "Homes are harder to find than in most places listed here. Much of the land is offices, hospital buildings and railway viaducts carrying trains into London Bridge and Cannon Street, so the residential stock is a scattering of mansion blocks, Victorian model-dwelling estates, small new-builds and converted warehouses. The exception is the Georgian terraces around Trinity Church Square, south of Borough station, which are among the most handsome streets in south London and very rarely come up to rent.",
    "Transport is almost too good to need. Borough station on the Northern line's Bank branch is small and less crowded than London Bridge, a few minutes' walk away with the Jubilee line and national rail in every direction. The honest downsides are noise from trains and nightlife, very limited green space beyond the cathedral gardens and small squares, and rents that reflect Zone 1. It suits people who value time and walkability over square footage.",
  ],

  "elephant-castle": [
    "Elephant and Castle has spent more than a decade being rebuilt, and moving here means accepting that it is not finished. The Heygate estate was demolished and replaced by Elephant Park, a development of new blocks around a central park; the 1960s shopping centre closed in 2020 and its site is being redeveloped as a new town centre with homes and a university campus. Towers such as Strata, with wind turbines in its roof, now define the skyline.",
    "What survived the rebuild is the area's mix. Elephant has long been a centre for London's Latin American community, and Colombian, Ecuadorian and Bolivian cafés, shops and hairdressers — many relocated from the old shopping centre — remain among its most distinctive features. To the south, Walworth Road and East Street Market feel like an older, busier south London high street.",
    "Transport is the headline. The Bakerloo line starts here, so you can get a seat to Waterloo, Oxford Circus and Paddington; the Northern line's Bank branch runs to London Bridge and the City; and Thameslink trains from the rail station cross the river to Blackfriars and St Pancras. The West End is a single bus ride or a longish walk.",
    "The downsides are physical. Major roads still meet at the junctions around the station, construction has been constant for years, and the old underpasses and roundabouts have been replaced in stages rather than all at once. Rents in the new towers run high for what is still a building site in places, while older estates and the Georgian and Victorian streets towards Kennington offer more character for similar money. Elephant suits people who want central transport and are comfortable living in a neighbourhood still in the middle of change.",
  ],

  camberwell: [
    "Camberwell is defined by two institutions and a lot of buses. Camberwell College of Arts brings a steady flow of students and artists, and King's College Hospital and the Maudsley at Denmark Hill are among the largest employers in south London, which is why so many doctors, nurses and researchers rent nearby. That mix gives the area its tone: creative, slightly scruffy, unpretentious.",
    "The centre around Camberwell Green is a busy crossroads, and it is not pretty. Climb the hill in almost any direction, though, and the streets change sharply. Camberwell Grove, rising south from Camberwell Church Street, is one of the finest Georgian streets in London, and the roads around it and towards Denmark Hill hold substantial Victorian villas, many split into flats. North and east of the Green, large council estates make up much of the housing.",
    "The food and pub scene punches well above the area's size, with small independent restaurants along Camberwell Church Street and Coldharbour Lane, and the pubs are the kind that fill with regulars on weeknights rather than stag parties. Burgess Park lies on the north-eastern edge and Ruskin Park sits beside Denmark Hill station.",
    "There is no tube, and the traffic through Camberwell Green is heavy enough that bus journeys to Elephant and Castle or Vauxhall can be quick at 6am and slow at 8. Denmark Hill station gives a reliable alternative: Overground trains towards Shoreditch and north London, Thameslink through Blackfriars, and Southeastern services to Victoria. People who settle here tend to work at the hospitals, cycle, or simply accept a slightly longer commute in exchange for rents below those in Dulwich and Brixton next door.",
  ],

  "east-dulwich": [
    "East Dulwich is a neighbourhood built for families that happens to rent well to couples and sharers. The streets were laid out in a rapid burst in the late Victorian and Edwardian years, and the result is remarkably uniform: long, straight roads of two-storey terraced houses with small gardens, many still whole houses, some split into upper and lower flats.",
    "Lordship Lane is the centre of gravity. Running south from Goose Green for well over a mile, it holds butchers, bakers, delis, bookshops, children's shops, cafés and a large number of pubs and restaurants, most of them independent. A small street market runs on North Cross Road on Saturdays. The atmosphere is relaxed and daytime-focused; the late-night scene is limited, and most bars are quiet by midnight.",
    "Parks are on every side. Peckham Rye Common and Park border the area to the east, Goose Green is a small green at its northern end, and Dulwich Park and Sydenham Hill Wood are within walking or cycling distance to the south. Dulwich Hamlet, a non-league football club with an unusually loyal local following, plays at Champion Hill.",
    "Transport is the recurring complaint. East Dulwich station, on Southern services to London Bridge, sits at the northern end of the area, so for anyone living towards the southern end of Lordship Lane it is a long walk. Many residents use Peckham Rye instead, for the Overground and faster trains, or rely on buses north towards Camberwell and the Elephant. The area suits people with a reasonably flexible commute who want a high street and parks that feel like a small town, and who are willing to pay for it despite having no tube.",
  ],

  // ── Camden ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  camden: [
    "Camden Town has two populations: the people who live here and the crowds who visit. From Friday to Sunday the stretch from the station to the canal is packed with market-goers heading for the Lock, the Stables and the food stalls along the Regent's Canal, and Camden High Street becomes difficult to walk along at any speed. Residents learn the back routes quickly.",
    "Music is the constant. The Roundhouse at Chalk Farm, the Electric Ballroom, the Jazz Cafe and a long list of pubs with back rooms mean there is a gig on most nights, and the late-night economy runs deep into the weekend. That energy is why people move here, and also why a flat on or near the high street can be very noisy.",
    "Step a few streets away and the area is more residential than its reputation suggests. Camden Square and the roads around it are handsome Victorian terraces; towards Primrose Hill the houses get grander, and to the east and north there are large council estates and newer canal-side blocks. Regent's Park, London Zoo and Primrose Hill are all within walking distance, and the canal towpath offers a quiet walk or cycle to King's Cross or Little Venice.",
    "Camden Town station is where the Northern line's two northern branches meet, so trains south to the West End and the City are frequent, but the station itself is cramped and gets congested at weekends. Kentish Town and Mornington Crescent are useful alternatives a short walk away. Camden suits people who want to be in the middle of things and do not mind sharing their neighbourhood with half of London every weekend.",
  ],

  "kentish-town": [
    "Kentish Town is where many people end up after deciding they like Camden but not living in it. It is one stop further up the Northern line, noticeably calmer, and has a different kind of high street: practical shops, supermarkets and a strong run of pubs and restaurants that serve locals rather than visitors.",
    "Its greatest asset is being at the foot of Hampstead Heath. Walk north-west for fifteen minutes through Dartmouth Park or Gospel Oak and you reach Parliament Hill, with one of the most famous views over London, the lido, and the whole expanse of the Heath beyond. Regular swimmers, runners and dog walkers are a big part of local life.",
    "The station is a real advantage. Kentish Town has both the Northern line's Edgware branch and Thameslink, which runs direct to St Pancras, Farringdon, Blackfriars and on to Gatwick and Brighton, and Kentish Town West adds the Overground towards Stratford and Richmond. The Forum, a large music venue by the station, brings crowds on gig nights but little disruption otherwise.",
    "The housing is mostly Victorian terraces, a large share of them converted into flats, with pockets of grand early-Victorian houses and several substantial council estates. Streets towards Dartmouth Park and Tufnell Park are the quietest and most sought-after; the roads immediately around the station and the high street are busier and more mixed. Kentish Town does not have Camden's buzz or Hampstead's prettiness, but it combines good transport, a real local scene and the Heath at rents a step below either, which is why it holds on to residents for years.",
  ],

  hampstead: [
    "Hampstead is a hilltop village that London grew around rather than over. Its centre is a knot of steep lanes, alleys and courtyards — Flask Walk, Church Row, Holly Walk — lined with Georgian houses, old pubs, small shops and cafés, and it keeps a village's sense of itself even as it has become one of the most expensive places to live in the city.",
    "Hampstead Heath is the reason for almost everything. The Heath is vast and deliberately wild in places, with woodland, meadows, the bathing ponds where swimmers go in all year, Kenwood House and its grounds at the northern end, and Parliament Hill's view over the city to the south-east. Living here means having a large piece of countryside at the end of the road.",
    "Hampstead station on the Northern line has the deepest platforms on the Underground, reached by lifts, and trains take around twenty minutes to the West End or the City. Hampstead Heath station at South End Green, on the Overground, gives a second route east and west. The hills are real: many streets are steep enough to make walking home with shopping a commitment.",
    "The rental market is dominated by flats in period houses and red-brick mansion blocks, plus some large family houses, with very little built for sharing. Evenings are quiet: there are pubs, a cinema and restaurants, but nightlife in any energetic sense means going elsewhere. The area is popular with families, older professionals and long-term residents, and the literary and artistic history — Keats House, Burgh House, Fenton House — is part of daily scenery rather than a museum trip. Hampstead suits people for whom space, beauty and the Heath outweigh value for money.",
  ],

  "west-hampstead": [
    "West Hampstead's defining feature is the cluster of stations on West End Lane. Within about two hundred metres there is the Jubilee line, the Overground and Thameslink, each with its own station, which means Bond Street and Waterloo, Stratford and Richmond, and St Pancras, Farringdon and Gatwick are all reachable without changing. Few places outside Zone 1 are this well connected.",
    "West End Lane itself is a lively, slightly traffic-heavy high street of cafés, restaurants, gyms, bars and small supermarkets, busy on weekday evenings with people coming home from work. The area has long been popular with young professionals and sharers — it once had a reputation as a first stop for Australians and New Zealanders arriving in London — and it still has that transitional, work-hard, brunch-at-weekends feel.",
    "Housing is dominated by red-brick Victorian and Edwardian mansion blocks and large terraced houses divided into flats, with a newer development of taller blocks around West Hampstead Square by the stations. The three railway lines run through the middle of the area in cuttings and on embankments, so some streets back onto tracks and freight trains pass through at night.",
    "Green space is closer than it looks on a map. Fortune Green and the adjoining Hampstead Cemetery are to the north, and Hampstead Heath is a twenty-minute walk or short bus ride uphill, though it is not on the doorstep in the way it is for Hampstead proper. West Hampstead suits people who commute, value choice of routes and want a busy high street, and who do not need their evenings to be especially quiet or their flat to be especially large for the money.",
  ],

  "belsize-park": [
    "Belsize Park sits on the slope between Hampstead and Primrose Hill, and it is defined by its architecture. Wide streets of large white stucco villas from the mid-nineteenth century, almost all now divided into flats, give way to red-brick mansion blocks towards Swiss Cottage. It is orderly, green and quiet in a way that reflects both money and design.",
    "Life centres on two small hubs. Haverstock Hill, around the tube station, has cafés, restaurants, a cinema and the kind of shops that serve a well-off residential population. Belsize Village, a short walk west, is a tiny cluster of shops and restaurants around a junction, surprisingly peaceful for somewhere two miles from Oxford Street.",
    "The location makes the area. Primrose Hill, with its view over Regent's Park to the city skyline, is a ten-minute walk south, Hampstead Heath is a similar distance north, and Camden's markets and music are downhill without being on the doorstep. The Royal Free Hospital at the top of Haverstock Hill is a major employer, and many medical staff rent locally.",
    "Belsize Park station is on the Northern line's Edgware branch and is one of the deep stations served only by lifts, which can mean a short wait at busy times; trains reach the West End in about fifteen minutes and the City via Camden. For the Jubilee line, residents walk to Swiss Cottage or Finchley Road. The rental market suits couples and professionals rather than sharers, and prices are high for the size of flat. People choose Belsize Park for calm, handsome streets and access to two of London's best open spaces, not for nightlife.",
  ],

  "swiss-cottage": [
    "Swiss Cottage takes its name from a chalet-style pub built on the Finchley Road in the nineteenth century, which still stands at the centre of the area's busy traffic junction. The pub is the most whimsical thing about the place: Swiss Cottage is essentially a residential district organised around major roads, with some of the quietest, wealthiest streets in north London a few minutes' walk away in every direction.",
    "The centre has an unusual civic cluster. The public library, designed by Basil Spence, sits beside a leisure centre, Hampstead Theatre and a small park with a market square. Finchley Road, running north, is a long shopping road with a large shopping and cinema complex at Finchley Road station.",
    "Transport is the main draw. Swiss Cottage is on the Jubilee line, which runs direct to Bond Street, Westminster, London Bridge and Canary Wharf, and Finchley Road station, ten minutes' walk up the hill, adds the Metropolitan line for a fast run to Baker Street. Regent's Park and Lord's are to the south in St John's Wood, and Primrose Hill is close.",
    "Housing is a mix of large mansion blocks, stucco villas turned into flats, modern apartment buildings and several estates, including the tall blocks of the Chalcots estate. The result is a quieter, more anonymous neighbourhood than Belsize Park or West Hampstead on either side: good for people who want professional, well-connected living without paying Hampstead or St John's Wood prices, less good for those who want a high street with character or an evening scene. The traffic on Finchley Road and the one-way system around the junction are the main daily irritation.",
  ],

  fitzrovia: [
    "Fitzrovia is the grid of streets between Oxford Street, Tottenham Court Road, Great Portland Street and Euston Road, and it combines being right in the centre of London with feeling surprisingly low-key. The BT Tower rises from its middle, and the area has long been home to media, advertising, architecture and tech offices, which fill its streets with people at lunchtime and empty them at weekends.",
    "Charlotte Street and the roads around it hold one of London's densest collections of restaurants, from long-established places to newer openings, and the pubs on its corners have literary and artistic histories. Fitzroy Square, at the northern end, is a Georgian square of pale stone terraces, one of the most elegant in the city.",
    "Homes here are mostly flats: above shops and restaurants, in mansion blocks, in converted Georgian and Victorian buildings, and in the new residential development built on the site of the old Middlesex Hospital. There are very few houses to share, and supply is small, so availability rather than just price is the challenge. University College Hospital sits on the eastern edge and many of its staff live nearby.",
    "Transport is abundant: Goodge Street and Warren Street on the Northern and Victoria lines, Tottenham Court Road with the Elizabeth and Central lines, and Great Portland Street for the Circle, Hammersmith & City and Metropolitan. Walking to the West End, Bloomsbury or King's Cross takes minutes. The trade-offs are the lack of green space beyond the small squares and nearby Regent's Park, the cost, and a neighbourhood that feels quiet and slightly empty on Sundays, when the offices close and the restaurants slow down. It suits people who work centrally and want to walk everywhere.",
  ],

  bloomsbury: [
    "Bloomsbury is London's intellectual quarter. The British Museum, University College London, SOAS, Senate House and a string of specialist institutes and libraries are all here, and the area is laid out around a series of Georgian garden squares — Russell, Bedford, Gordon, Tavistock, Brunswick — that give it an unusually calm, planned feel for somewhere in the middle of the city.",
    "Many of the terraces around those squares are now university departments, hotels or offices, so the residential stock is smaller than the architecture implies. Rentals are mostly flats in mansion blocks, in converted Georgian houses and in the Brunswick Centre, a large stepped modernist housing and shopping complex that is one of the area's landmarks. Student halls take up a significant share of the housing, and in term time the population is young.",
    "Local life is gentle. Lamb's Conduit Street has independent shops, a few restaurants and a well-known pub, and Marchmont Street serves the area around Brunswick Square. Coram's Fields, a large playground and park, is famously open to adults only if they are accompanied by a child. There is good green space in the squares, though most are small, and Regent's Park is a fifteen-minute walk away.",
    "Transport is excellent. Russell Square on the Piccadilly line runs direct to King's Cross and to the West End, Holborn and Tottenham Court Road give the Central and Elizabeth lines, and King's Cross St Pancras, a walk away, adds almost everything else plus Eurostar. Bloomsbury suits academics, students, hospital and university staff, and professionals who want a central, walkable and quiet base. Those looking for nightlife walk to Soho or King's Cross; Bloomsbury itself is mostly asleep by eleven.",
  ],

  // ── Westminster ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  pimlico: [
    "Pimlico was laid out in the mid-nineteenth century by the builder Thomas Cubitt as a regular grid of cream stucco terraces, and it still looks remarkably uniform: long, straight streets, white porticoes, black railings and garden squares. It sits between Victoria station and the Thames, and its layout keeps through traffic mostly on the edges, which makes it one of the quietest parts of central London.",
    "Behind the stucco the housing is more varied than it looks. Most of the terraced houses are divided into flats, many of them small; Dolphin Square, a huge 1930s block of flats with its own gardens and pool, is a Pimlico institution; and Churchill Gardens, a post-war council estate by the river, was one of the most admired social housing schemes of its time. A significant share of homes are second residences or short lets for people working in Westminster, and parts of the area can feel empty in August.",
    "Everyday life is quietly convenient. Tachbrook Street has a small market, Warwick Way has shops and cafés, and Tate Britain sits on the riverside at Millbank. The Thames Path runs along the southern edge, and the walk across Vauxhall Bridge or along to Westminster is easy.",
    "Pimlico station on the Victoria line is the area's only tube stop, and it is quick: Victoria in one stop, Oxford Circus in a few more. Victoria mainline and coach stations are a short walk for trains to Gatwick and the south coast. The area suits civil servants, parliamentary staff and professionals who want to walk to work, and people who want calm streets in Zone 1. It is not the place for nightlife, big supermarkets or a strong sense of local buzz.",
  ],

  bayswater: [
    "Bayswater runs along the northern side of Hyde Park and Kensington Gardens, and that gives it one of the best front gardens in London. The streets behind the park are lined with grand white stucco terraces from the Victorian building boom; many were long ago converted into hotels, bedsits, studios and small flats, which is why Bayswater has more compact, relatively affordable central rentals than its architecture suggests.",
    "Queensway is the busy centre: a long strip of restaurants and shops with a very international character, reflecting decades of arrivals from the Middle East, Asia and elsewhere, and a large new development at the old Whiteleys department store. Westbourne Grove, heading towards Notting Hill, has a smarter mix of shops and cafés.",
    "The area is constantly busy. Hotels and short-term lets bring a steady flow of visitors, particularly around Queensway and the streets near Paddington, and the population turns over faster than in most places. Some squares and crescents are very quiet and well-kept, while other streets feel tired and transient; the difference can be one block.",
    "Transport is a strength. Bayswater station has the Circle and District lines, Queensway and Lancaster Gate are on the Central line for a fast run to Oxford Circus and the City, and Paddington, a short walk east, adds the Elizabeth line, the Bakerloo and the Heathrow Express. Living here means Hyde Park for running, cycling and summer evenings, the West End in ten minutes, and Heathrow within easy reach. It suits people who want central London and a park on the doorstep and will accept a small flat, some bustle and a less settled neighbourhood in return.",
  ],

  "maida-vale": [
    "Maida Vale is a district of wide, tree-lined avenues and red-brick mansion blocks, most of them put up around the turn of the twentieth century, and it has the settled, slightly formal atmosphere that such buildings create. Elgin Avenue, Sutherland Avenue and the streets around them are among the most complete runs of mansion flats in London, many with shared private gardens behind.",
    "At its southern end is Little Venice, where the Regent's Canal meets the Grand Union Canal in a pool lined with houseboats, a floating puppet theatre and waterside cafés. The towpath heads east to Regent's Park and Camden and west towards Kensal Green, and it is one of the pleasantest walking and cycling routes in central London.",
    "Daily life is quiet and convenient rather than lively. Local shops cluster around Clifton Road, near Warwick Avenue station, and along Elgin Avenue, and there are good pubs, but nightlife means heading into the West End. Paddington Recreation Ground, a large park with sports facilities, is on the western edge, and Regent's Park is within reach.",
    "The Bakerloo line is the area's tube, with stations at Maida Vale and Warwick Avenue; it runs direct to Paddington, Oxford Circus and Waterloo, but it is slower and older than lines elsewhere, and there is no quick direct route to the City. Buses along Edgware Road and Elgin Avenue fill the gaps, and Paddington's Elizabeth line is a short trip away. The rental market is overwhelmingly flats in mansion blocks and converted houses, which suits couples and professionals more than large sharing groups. Maida Vale is for people who want central London to feel calm and residential, and who do not need a buzzing high street outside the front door.",
  ],

  soho: [
    "Living in Soho means living in London's most concentrated entertainment district, and almost nothing about it is designed for residents. Its narrow streets hold hundreds of restaurants, bars, pubs, clubs and theatres, along with the film and post-production companies that have been based here for decades. Old Compton Street remains the centre of London's LGBTQ+ scene, and Chinatown borders it to the south.",
    "People do live here, though, and more than visitors realise. Flats above shops and restaurants, a handful of mansion blocks and a significant stock of housing association homes keep a resident community that has fought for decades to protect Soho's character. Berwick Street's long-running street market and small squares like Soho Square and Golden Square give the neighbourhood some everyday texture.",
    "The noise is constant and varied. Bars close late, crowds spill onto streets on most nights, deliveries and bin collections start early in the morning, and a flat that is quiet on a Tuesday at 4pm can be very different on a Saturday at 1am. Double glazing, a rear-facing bedroom and a high floor matter far more here than anywhere else on this site.",
    "In return, everything is within walking distance. Tottenham Court Road has the Elizabeth, Central and Northern lines, and Oxford Circus, Piccadilly Circus and Leicester Square are all minutes away; many residents rarely use transport at all. Green space is almost absent beyond the small squares, and Hyde Park or St James's Park are the nearest large ones. Soho suits people who work in the West End or in hospitality and the creative industries, who want the city at their door and are willing to pay heavily for a small flat and live with the noise that comes with it.",
  ],

  mayfair: [
    "Mayfair is the most expensive residential area on this site, and the price reflects centuries of development by a few aristocratic estates, most notably the Grosvenor estate. Its Georgian and Victorian streets and squares — Grosvenor Square, Berkeley Square, Mount Street — are home to embassies, hedge funds, private members' clubs, luxury hotels and some of the most exclusive shops in the world along New Bond Street and Savile Row.",
    "The residential population is small and often absent. Many homes are second or third residences, corporate lets or serviced apartments, and the flats that come up to rent are mostly in mansion blocks or converted period buildings, often professionally let and furnished. Rents per square foot are among the highest in London, and it is common to see very small flats listed at prices that would rent a family house elsewhere.",
    "Shepherd Market, a tiny cluster of lanes with pubs and restaurants between Curzon Street and Piccadilly, is the closest Mayfair comes to a village centre. Everyday shopping is harder than you would expect: there are few supermarkets and many of the shops sell things residents buy rarely. Green Park and Hyde Park border the area, which is an enormous advantage for running, walking and quiet.",
    "Transport and walkability are outstanding. Green Park has the Jubilee, Piccadilly and Victoria lines, Bond Street has the Central, Jubilee and Elizabeth lines, and Oxford Street, Soho and St James's are all a walk away. Mayfair suits people with corporate relocation packages, very high incomes or a need to be at a particular office or embassy, and it rarely makes sense for younger renters trying to get value from their budget.",
  ],

  "marylebone-area": [
    "Marylebone feels like a smart village that happens to be a few minutes from Oxford Street. Much of it is owned and managed by two historic estates, Howard de Walden and Portman, and that long-term management shows: Marylebone High Street is curated with independent shops, bookshops, cafés, delis and restaurants, and the streets around it are kept consistent and well maintained.",
    "The area has distinct characters within it. The north is dominated by Regent's Park, the grand terraces around it and the Marylebone Road; Harley Street and Wimpole Street are famous for private medical practices; and the streets west towards Edgware Road and south towards the Wallace Collection and Manchester Square are quieter and residential. Wigmore Hall, one of the world's leading chamber music venues, is on the southern side.",
    "Housing is mostly mansion flats and Georgian and Victorian buildings divided into apartments, with a small number of mews houses. Flats are generally smaller and more expensive than elsewhere in Zone 1, and the rental market is geared towards professionals and corporate tenants rather than house shares.",
    "Transport is excellent. Baker Street has five tube lines, Bond Street adds the Central, Jubilee and Elizabeth lines, and Marylebone station runs Chiltern Railways trains towards Oxford and Birmingham. Regent's Park, one of the most beautiful central parks, is on the doorstep, as is the canal. The trade-offs are cost and tone: Marylebone's evenings are refined, with restaurants and wine bars rather than clubs, and the area appeals to people who want central London to feel polished and calm. It is a good fit for doctors, professionals and anyone who works in the West End and wants to walk there.",
  ],

  "covent-garden": [
    "Covent Garden is theatreland and a tourist destination first, and a place to live second. The old market building and piazza, the Royal Opera House, street performers, dozens of theatres and hundreds of restaurants and shops draw crowds every day of the year, from morning to late at night.",
    "Its residential community is nonetheless real and has a notable history. In the 1970s, when the fruit and vegetable market moved out, plans to demolish much of the area were defeated by a local campaign, and that fight left behind housing co-operatives and housing association homes that still house long-standing residents. Today most flats are above shops and restaurants, in mansion blocks or in converted warehouses, and new private flats are among the most expensive in London.",
    "Some pockets offer relative calm. Seven Dials and Neal's Yard, while busy, are more boutique than the piazza, and the streets towards Holborn and around Drury Lane are quieter at night. Otherwise, noise from crowds, performers, deliveries and late-closing venues is part of daily life, and green space is almost non-existent; the Victoria Embankment Gardens and the river are the nearest open spaces.",
    "Covent Garden station on the Piccadilly line is small and served only by lifts, and at busy times it is often quicker to walk to Leicester Square, Holborn or Tottenham Court Road; almost everything else in the West End is reachable on foot. Charing Cross and Waterloo, a short walk away, offer rail connections south. Covent Garden suits people who work in theatre, hospitality or the West End, who want to live at the centre of London's cultural life and accept high rents, small flats and constant activity as the price of that.",
  ],

  // ── Haringey ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  "crouch-end": [
    "Crouch End sits in a hollow between hills, and its centre is marked by a Victorian clock tower at the junction of its main shopping streets. Around it is one of north London's most complete small-town high streets: independent bakers, butchers, bookshops, cafés, restaurants and pubs, busy in the daytime with parents, freelancers and people who have chosen to work locally.",
    "The area never got an Underground station, and that absence shapes everything. The nearest tubes are Highgate, Archway and Finsbury Park, each a bus ride or a long walk away, and many residents take a bus to Finsbury Park for the Victoria and Piccadilly lines. Hornsey and Harringay stations on the Great Northern line and Crouch Hill on the Overground help, but none is in the centre. The upside is fewer through-crowds and a slower pace.",
    "Green space is a major strength. The Parkland Walk, a disused railway line turned into a long wooded path, runs from Finsbury Park towards Highgate along the area's edge; Priory Park is popular with families; and Alexandra Palace and its park, with sweeping views over London, are a short walk uphill to the north. Hornsey Town Hall, a 1930s modernist landmark on the Broadway, has been restored and converted to new uses.",
    "The housing is mostly late Victorian and Edwardian terraced houses and mansion flats, many converted into flats but many still family homes. That is why Crouch End leans towards families, creative professionals and couples in their thirties and forties, and why sharers are a smaller part of the market. Evenings are relaxed — good pubs and restaurants, little late-night life. It suits people who value a real local centre and space over speed, and who can live with a two-stage commute.",
  ],

  "finsbury-park": [
    "Finsbury Park is a transport hub with a neighbourhood attached. The station has the Victoria and Piccadilly lines plus Great Northern and Thameslink services, so Oxford Circus, King's Cross and the City are all a few minutes away, and it is one of the best-connected places in north London for the money. That convenience is the main reason people move here.",
    "The park itself is large and well used, with sports pitches, a lake, a running track and space for the major music events held there each summer, which bring big crowds and noise for a few weekends a year. The Parkland Walk starts at its edge. Arsenal's stadium is just to the south in Highbury, and match days fill the streets and the station with fans.",
    "The area is where three boroughs meet — Haringey, Islington and Hackney — and it feels like a meeting point. Stroud Green Road, running north from the station, has a strong mix of cafés, independent restaurants, bars and grocers, while Blackstock Road and Seven Sisters Road reflect North African, Turkish and many other communities. Some parts are busy, noisy and a little rough around the station; others, especially towards Stroud Green, are quieter and greener.",
    "Housing is a mix of large Victorian terraces converted into flats, mansion blocks, estates and some new developments near the station. The rental market is large and varied, and rents are generally below neighbouring Islington or Highbury while the commute is as good or better. Finsbury Park suits people who put transport first, want a big park nearby and are happy with a lively, sometimes gritty environment. Those looking for a pretty village centre tend to move on to Crouch End or Stoke Newington once they settle.",
  ],

  highgate: [
    "Highgate is a hilltop village in two parts, and the difference matters. The historic village, around Highgate High Street and Pond Square, is a cluster of Georgian houses, old pubs, small shops and a famous school at the top of the hill; the Underground station, on the Northern line's High Barnet branch, sits further down on Archway Road, a busy main road. Walking between them takes about ten minutes and involves a proper climb.",
    "Green space surrounds the area on almost every side. Hampstead Heath and Kenwood are to the west, Waterlow Park with Lauderdale House is on the hillside below the village, and Highgate Wood and Queen's Wood, both ancient woodland, lie to the north. Highgate Cemetery, with its Victorian catacombs and the grave of Karl Marx, is one of London's most atmospheric places and still an active cemetery.",
    "The housing stock is unusually distinguished. Georgian and Victorian houses fill the village and its lanes, there are substantial Edwardian and interwar homes, and Highpoint, a pair of 1930s modernist apartment blocks by Berthold Lubetkin on North Hill, is one of the most celebrated examples of the style in Britain. Rental supply is limited, and much of it is flats in larger houses or mansion blocks.",
    "Daily life is quiet and orderly. The village has pubs, restaurants and cafés but no nightlife to speak of, and bigger shopping trips mean Archway, Muswell Hill or further afield. Highgate sits across the borders of Haringey, Camden and Islington, which can matter for council services and schools. It suits families, established professionals and people who want to feel close to the countryside while keeping a tube line, and who do not mind a steep walk home.",
  ],

  "muswell-hill": [
    "Muswell Hill is one of the most complete Edwardian suburbs in London. Almost all of it was built in a rapid burst in the years either side of 1900, so its central Broadway is lined with unusually grand parades of shops with ornate upper floors, and the streets around it are filled with large, well-built terraced and semi-detached houses on steep slopes.",
    "Its setting is exceptional. Alexandra Palace and its park sit on the next hill to the east, with one of the best panoramic views over London, a boating lake, ice rink and a venue that hosts concerts and events. Highgate Wood and Queen's Wood are to the south-west, and Coldfall Wood to the north. Many streets have long views across the city.",
    "Transport is the obvious weakness. The area lost its railway station decades ago and has no tube; getting anywhere usually starts with a bus to Highgate or East Finchley on the Northern line, to Bounds Green on the Piccadilly, or down to Finsbury Park. Alexandra Palace station, on the Great Northern line, is at the bottom of the hill. Journeys into central London are rarely quick, and the hills make cycling hard work.",
    "In return, residents get space, quiet and a strong local centre: independent shops, cafés, restaurants and a cinema on the Broadway, and some of the most sought-after state schools in north London. That makes the area strongly family-oriented, with a settled population and a rental market dominated by flats in converted houses and whole family homes rather than house shares. Evenings are calm. Muswell Hill suits people who work flexibly or locally, or who will accept a longer commute for greenery, well-built homes and a village atmosphere.",
  ],

  "wood-green": [
    "Wood Green is a working town centre rather than a village. The High Road is one of north London's biggest shopping streets, with a large shopping mall that bridges the road, national chains, discount stores, markets and plenty of places to eat. It is busy and practical, and it is the place people from the surrounding hillside neighbourhoods come to for everyday errands.",
    "The area has a notably diverse population, with long-established Greek Cypriot and Turkish Cypriot communities, and more recent arrivals from Eastern Europe, Africa and Latin America. Green Lanes, running south through Harringay, is famous for its long strip of Turkish and Kurdish restaurants, grills and bakeries, many open late.",
    "Transport is straightforward and quick. Wood Green and Turnpike Lane stations are on the Piccadilly line, which reaches King's Cross in around fifteen minutes and continues to the West End, and the Great Northern trains at Alexandra Palace are a short walk away. Bus routes run in every direction. Alexandra Palace and its park rise immediately to the west of the High Road, which gives the area a major green space and viewpoint very close by.",
    "Housing is a mix of Victorian and Edwardian terraces, many converted into flats, post-war estates and a growing number of new-build developments, including large schemes on former industrial and gasworks land. Rents are typically noticeably lower than in Crouch End, Muswell Hill or Highgate just up the hill, with a better tube connection than any of them. The trade-offs are a busy, unglamorous centre and limited evening life beyond restaurants. Wood Green suits people who want value and a direct tube line, and who are happy to walk up the hill for the prettier parts of north London.",
  ],

  tottenham: [
    "Tottenham is a large and varied part of north London, and where exactly you live within it matters more than almost anywhere else. The High Road runs north from Seven Sisters past the Tottenham Hotspur Stadium, whose arrival in 2019 brought regeneration, match-day crowds and new businesses; to the east, Tottenham Hale has been transformed by new residential towers next to its station.",
    "Transport is strong for north-east London. Tottenham Hale and Seven Sisters are both on the Victoria line, which reaches King's Cross in under fifteen minutes and Oxford Circus soon after, and Tottenham Hale adds Greater Anglia trains to Liverpool Street and the Stansted Express. The further you are from those stations, though, the more the commute depends on buses along the congested High Road.",
    "One of Tottenham's biggest assets is the Lee Valley on its eastern edge. Tottenham Marshes, the River Lea towpath and the vast Walthamstow Wetlands nature reserve are close to Tottenham Hale, giving easy access to open space, birdwatching and long walks or cycle routes north into Hertfordshire and south to the Olympic Park.",
    "The area is among the most diverse in the country, with large African, Caribbean, Turkish, Kurdish, Eastern European and Latin American communities, and it has strong local institutions such as Bruce Castle museum and the Bernie Grant Arts Centre. Housing ranges from Victorian terraces and the early twentieth-century cottage estate of Tower Gardens to large post-war estates and new-build blocks near the Hale. Some streets feel neglected, and the public realm is uneven. Tottenham suits people who prioritise value, a fast Victoria line connection and access to the Lee Valley, and who choose their exact street carefully.",
  ],

  // ── Lewisham ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  "new-cross": [
    "New Cross is shaped by Goldsmiths, University of London, whose campus sits between the two stations. Its students and staff give the area a young, creative and slightly anarchic character, and its alumni list of artists and musicians partly explains why the pubs and small venues along New Cross Road host so much live music and so many late nights.",
    "The main roads are the area's biggest downside. New Cross Road carries heavy traffic through the centre, and the junctions around the stations are noisy and polluted, which makes the high street feel more like a through route than a destination. The best places to live are away from it.",
    "Telegraph Hill, south of the main road, is the obvious example: a conservation area of large Victorian houses on steep streets around two hilltop parks, from which there are long views over central London. Houses here are often split into flats, and the neighbourhood has a strong community feel, with a well-known local festival. Elsewhere, housing is a mix of Victorian terraces, large estates and purpose-built student accommodation.",
    "Transport is better than it first appears. New Cross station has Southeastern trains to London Bridge and Cannon Street in under ten minutes, and both New Cross and New Cross Gate have Overground trains north through Canada Water and Shoreditch towards Dalston and Highbury. New Cross Gate also has Southern services to London Bridge. There is no tube. New Cross suits students, young creatives and people who want Zone 2 access to London Bridge at rents below Peckham and Deptford, and who do not mind busy roads and a rough edge in places.",
  ],

  deptford: [
    "Deptford has a longer history than most of London. It was the site of a royal dockyard founded under Henry VIII, and the London and Greenwich Railway, the capital's first, ran its earliest trains between Deptford and Spa Road in 1836. Traces of that past are everywhere, from St Paul's, one of London's finest baroque churches, to the riverside site of the old dockyard, now being redeveloped.",
    "Today the area is best known for its high street and its creative scene. Deptford High Street is a traditional, unpretentious market street, with a street market on several days a week, pie-and-mash and cheap everyday shopping alongside newer cafés and restaurants. Deptford Market Yard, in restored railway arches by the station, adds small independent food and drink businesses. Creekside is home to Trinity Laban, whose dance building is an award-winning modern landmark, and studios and galleries cluster nearby.",
    "The housing mix is broad: Victorian terraces, large post-war estates, including the riverside Pepys estate, and growing numbers of new-build flats close to the river and the station. The streets vary quickly, and some parts feel rundown, while others are quiet and residential. Greenwich, with its park and river, is a short walk east.",
    "Deptford station has Southeastern trains to London Bridge in around ten minutes and on to Cannon Street and Charing Cross, and Deptford Bridge on the DLR runs to Canary Wharf, Bank and Lewisham. There is no tube, and evening trains are less frequent. Deptford suits people who want character, a real market street and a creative community close to Greenwich and the City, at rents below its more polished neighbour.",
  ],

  lewisham: [
    "Lewisham is a major town centre and transport interchange, and its appeal is practical. The station combines the DLR, which starts here and runs to Canary Wharf and Bank, with Southeastern trains that reach London Bridge in under ten minutes and continue to Cannon Street, Charing Cross and sometimes Victoria. For the price, very few places give such fast access to both the City and Docklands.",
    "The centre has been rebuilt around the station in recent years, with tall residential towers, new public spaces and a rerouted road layout, alongside the older shopping centre and a long-running street market on the High Street. It is busy, functional and diverse rather than charming, and large chain shops and supermarkets make everyday life easy.",
    "Two small rivers, the Ravensbourne and the Quaggy, meet near the centre, and some of the area's best green space follows their valleys. Ladywell Fields, restored as a riverside park, runs south towards Catford; Hilly Fields, with views across south-east London, sits on the ridge towards Brockley; and Blackheath is a short walk uphill to the east. University Hospital Lewisham is a major local employer.",
    "The housing stock is split between new-build flats in and around the town centre, which are generally modern but can be small, and Victorian and Edwardian terraces in the surrounding neighbourhoods of Ladywell, Hither Green and Lee, which are quieter and more residential. For an evening out, residents mostly head to Brockley or Deptford; the town centre itself winds down early. Lewisham suits commuters working at Canary Wharf, London Bridge or the City who want value and choice of routes, and who can accept a busy, sometimes unlovely town centre.",
  ],

  catford: [
    "Catford is a south-east London town centre that is known, first of all, for a large fibreglass cat that climbs across the entrance to its shopping centre. It is an appropriately unpretentious symbol for a place that has long been more practical than fashionable, and where the local council has plans to overhaul a town centre dominated by the South Circular road.",
    "The civic and cultural centre includes the Broadway Theatre, an art deco building that hosts performances and community events, alongside the town hall, markets and a mix of shops. The traffic on the South Circular, which runs straight through the centre, is the area's biggest daily nuisance.",
    "Some of the most appealing streets are a short walk away. The Corbett Estate, towards Hither Green, is a large and uniform grid of Edwardian terraced houses originally built for clerks and skilled workers, now popular with families. Ladywell Fields runs north towards Lewisham along the River Ravensbourne, and Mountsfield Park, on the hill to the east, has one of the area's best views and a community garden.",
    "Catford has two stations close together. Catford Bridge has Southeastern trains towards London Bridge and Cannon Street, and Catford has Thameslink services through Elephant and Castle and Blackfriars to St Pancras; neither line runs as often as a tube. Bus routes connect to Lewisham, Brockley and beyond. Rents are generally below those in Brockley, Forest Hill and Lewisham itself. The area suits families and renters seeking more space for their money, and people with reasonably flexible commutes who are happy to trade nightlife and polish for value and access to good local parks.",
  ],

  blackheath: [
    "Blackheath is named after the wide, open heath that sits at its heart: a large, almost treeless plateau crossed by roads, with All Saints' Church standing alone on its southern side. Kite-flyers, walkers and runners use it every day, and on one Sunday each spring it is where the London Marathon begins. Greenwich Park adjoins the heath to the north, so the area has an enormous amount of open space.",
    "The village, clustered below the heath around the station, has a traditional high street of independent shops, cafés, restaurants and pubs, plus Blackheath Halls, a concert venue. It feels polished and well kept, and is noticeably quieter than Greenwich on busy weekends.",
    "Housing is one of Blackheath's defining attractions. The Paragon, a sweeping crescent of Georgian houses joined by colonnades, is one of the most admired terraces in London, and handsome Georgian and Victorian villas line the heath. Blackheath also has an unusually large number of mid-twentieth-century Span estates, modernist developments of houses and flats set in shared landscaped gardens, which are prized by architecture enthusiasts. The rental market is limited and mostly flats in period houses or those estates.",
    "Blackheath station has Southeastern trains to London Bridge, Cannon Street and Charing Cross, taking around fifteen to twenty minutes to London Bridge; there is no tube or DLR in the village, though Lewisham's DLR is a short bus ride or walk away. The area sits across the borders of Lewisham and Greenwich. Blackheath is for households who want space, beautiful architecture and village life within easy reach of central London, and who are happy with rail-based commuting and quiet evenings.",
  ],

  "forest-hill": [
    "Forest Hill is a steep, leafy suburb on the ridge of hills south-east of Dulwich, and its best-known institution is the Horniman Museum, whose collections range from natural history and musical instruments to an aquarium. The museum's hillside gardens, with a bandstand and one of south London's best views towards the City, are free and busy with local families.",
    "The area's green character extends beyond the Horniman. Sydenham Hill Wood and Dulwich Wood, remnants of the ancient Great North Wood, are within walking distance, and many streets have mature trees and large gardens. The hills are part of the appeal and a genuine obstacle: walking or cycling home from the station can be hard work.",
    "The housing includes large Victorian villas, many divided into flats, Edwardian terraces and some interwar and post-war blocks. It often offers more space for the rent than Dulwich or East Dulwich nearby, which explains the steady arrival of young families and couples priced out of those areas. Local shops and cafés gather around the station and along Dartmouth Road, where the restored Victorian swimming baths are a local landmark.",
    "Forest Hill station is on the Overground, running up through Surrey Quays and Whitechapel towards Dalston and Highbury, and Southern services to London Bridge in around fifteen minutes. That makes east London and the City accessible, but the West End and west London are slower, typically requiring a change. The South Circular road cuts through the area and can be congested, and evenings revolve around a handful of pubs. Forest Hill suits people who value green space, larger homes and a relaxed pace, and whose commute fits the Overground or London Bridge.",
  ],

  // ── Hackney ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  "stoke-newington": [
    "Stoke Newington has two main streets with very different personalities. Church Street, running west from the High Street, is the village: independent bookshops, delis, cafés, children's shops, restaurants and pubs, with old Georgian buildings mixed in. Stoke Newington High Street, part of the long A10 route north, is busier and louder, lined with Turkish restaurants, grocers and late-opening shops.",
    "Its green spaces are unusually varied. Clissold Park, with its eighteenth-century house, ponds, animal enclosure and sports areas, is the social centre in summer. Abney Park, a Victorian garden cemetery allowed to grow wild, is now a woodland nature reserve criss-crossed by paths. To the north, the West Reservoir is used for open-water swimming and sailing, and Woodberry Wetlands, on the East Reservoir, is a nature reserve with a café.",
    "The population is mixed and settled. Families and creative professionals have been moving in for decades, alongside long-established Turkish, Kurdish and Caribbean communities, and the large Orthodox Jewish community of Stamford Hill begins just to the north. The housing is predominantly Victorian terraced houses, a large share converted into flats, with some Georgian houses near Church Street and several council estates.",
    "There is no tube, and that is the recurring compromise. Stoke Newington and Rectory Road stations are on the Overground line from Liverpool Street to Enfield and Cheshunt, which reaches Liverpool Street in around fifteen minutes but can be crowded at peak times. Many residents cycle, or take a bus south to Dalston, Highbury or Old Street for other lines. Evenings are relaxed: good restaurants and pubs, but little late-night activity. Stoke Newington suits people who want an established neighbourhood with independent shops and parks, and who can live with an Overground commute.",
  ],

  "hackney-central": [
    "Hackney Central is the civic heart of the borough. Mare Street runs through it past the art deco town hall and the Hackney Empire, an ornate Edwardian music hall that still stages everything from comedy to pantomime, and the streets around the station are busy with shops, markets, cafés and a steady flow of buses.",
    "Much of what made Hackney fashionable sits within a short walk. London Fields, with its heated outdoor lido, is to the south-west, and Broadway Market, on its southern edge, is packed on Saturdays with food stalls and shoppers. The railway arches between Hackney Central and London Fields hold bars, bakeries, workshops and breweries, and Well Street and Chatsworth Road offer quieter, more local versions of the same mix. Victoria Park and Hackney Downs are close.",
    "There is no tube, but two Overground stations sit almost next to each other. Hackney Downs has trains to Liverpool Street in around ten minutes, while Hackney Central is on the east–west line from Stratford to Highbury, Camden and Richmond. The combination works well for the City, Shoreditch and Stratford, less well for the West End, which usually needs a change.",
    "The housing includes Victorian terraces, mostly converted into flats, large post-war estates, warehouse and factory conversions and newer blocks near the stations. Rents rose sharply in the last decade as the area became a byword for creative east London, and long-standing communities — including large Caribbean, Turkish and African populations — have felt that pressure. Hackney Central suits people who want food, nightlife, markets and parks close at hand and are happy with Overground commuting, and who choose their street with care, since character and noise levels change quickly from one block to the next.",
  ],

  dalston: [
    "Dalston is loud, crowded and busy at almost every hour, and that is exactly its appeal. Ridley Road Market, with its fruit, vegetables, fish, fabrics and hair products, runs most days just off Kingsland High Street, while the stretch of Kingsland Road and Stoke Newington Road to the north is lined with Turkish and Kurdish grill restaurants, many open late into the night.",
    "The nightlife is among the most concentrated in London. Bars, small clubs, music venues and late-opening restaurants cluster around Kingsland High Street and the side streets, and weekends bring large crowds from across the city. The Rio, an art deco cinema, and the Arcola Theatre add some culture. The Eastern Curve Garden, a community garden built on an old railway line behind the high street, is a small, much-loved green refuge.",
    "Transport is all Overground but covers a lot of ground. Dalston Junction is on the north–south line through Shoreditch and Whitechapel to Canada Water, Crystal Palace, Clapham Junction and New Cross, and Dalston Kingsland, a short walk away, has trains west towards Camden and Willesden and east to Stratford. For the tube, residents typically take a bus to Highbury & Islington or Old Street.",
    "Housing ranges from new-build towers around Dalston Square, near the Junction station, to Victorian terraces and conversions and substantial council estates, with the handsome squares of De Beauvoir Town a short walk south-west. Many flats are close to busy roads and nightlife, which means noise is a genuine consideration, and green space nearby is limited, with the nearest large parks being Clissold Park, London Fields and Hackney Downs. Dalston suits young people who want food and nightlife on their doorstep, and who value that over quiet or a leafy street.",
  ],

  shoreditch: [
    "Shoreditch straddles the boundary between Hackney and Tower Hamlets on the edge of the City of London, and it is the densest concentration of bars, restaurants, clubs, galleries, tech offices and street art in east London. Office towers from the City press in from the south, and on weekday evenings workers pour out into the bars; at weekends the crowds come from everywhere.",
    "The area has distinct parts. Around Old Street roundabout, known for years as Silicon Roundabout, tech and design companies dominate; Shoreditch High Street and Great Eastern Street are the nightlife core; Brick Lane, to the south-east, is famous for its curry houses, vintage shops and Sunday markets; and Columbia Road, to the north-east, fills with its flower market on Sunday mornings. The Boundary Estate, built in the 1890s around the raised garden of Arnold Circus, was one of the first council housing schemes in the world and is still lived in.",
    "Housing is a mixture of warehouse and loft conversions, new luxury towers, flats above shops, older council estates and that historic estate. Very little of it is quiet, and rents are among the highest in east London, especially for new-build or converted warehouse flats with outdoor space.",
    "Transport is outstanding. Liverpool Street, with the Elizabeth line, Central line and national rail services, is a walk away; Old Street has the Northern line; and Shoreditch High Street station is on the Overground. Almost the whole of central London is within a short journey, and the City is walkable. The trade-offs are noise, crowds, very little green space beyond small squares and Shoreditch Park, and the cost. It is a place for City and tech workers who want to live where the social life is, not for anyone looking for calm.",
  ],

  hoxton: [
    "Hoxton sits immediately north of Shoreditch, and it shares much of that area's energy while feeling more residential. Hoxton Square, surrounded by bars, galleries and restaurants, is the social centre, and Hoxton Street, running north, has a traditional street market on Saturdays and a mix of cafés, shops and long-established local businesses.",
    "Kingsland Road, forming the area's eastern edge, is known for its long run of Vietnamese restaurants, and also for the Museum of the Home, housed in a row of eighteenth-century almshouses with period gardens behind them. The Regent's Canal runs along the northern boundary, with its towpath providing a traffic-free route east to Victoria Park or west to Islington.",
    "Much of Hoxton's housing is social housing, in large post-war estates, alongside Victorian terraces, converted warehouses and new private developments. That mix means it has kept more of its long-standing working-class community than Shoreditch, and some parts feel much quieter than others. Shoreditch Park provides the main green space in the neighbourhood.",
    "Hoxton station is on the Overground line through Shoreditch and Whitechapel, heading north to Dalston and Highbury and south to Canada Water and beyond. Old Street, on the Northern line and national rail, is a walk of ten to fifteen minutes from the southern parts of Hoxton, and Liverpool Street is not much further. The nightlife around Hoxton Square and along Old Street can be noisy at weekends, especially close to the square itself. Hoxton suits young professionals and creatives who want to be close to Shoreditch's bars and the City, but prefer a slightly more residential base and are happy to walk to the nearest tube.",
  ],

  "hackney-wick": [
    "Hackney Wick is an industrial island in the Lea Valley, cut off from the rest of Hackney by the A12 and bounded by the River Lee Navigation and the Hertford Union Canal. For years its warehouses and yards housed one of the largest concentrations of artists' studios in Europe, and the area is still known for studios, music venues, breweries, bars and warehouse events, though many former industrial buildings have been replaced by new-build flats.",
    "The Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park lies directly across the canal, and it is the area's main open space. Its riverside paths, lawns and playgrounds connect to the London Stadium, the Aquatics Centre, and East Bank, the new cultural and education quarter with museums, a theatre and university campuses. Hackney Marshes, with its famous football pitches, stretches north along the river.",
    "The neighbourhood is mostly flats: canal-side new builds with balconies, converted warehouses, live-work units and some older terraces. The streets can feel half-finished, with construction still ongoing, and some parts are empty at night. Much of the area's social life happens by the water, especially on summer evenings.",
    "Hackney Wick station is on the Overground line between Stratford and Highbury, Camden and Richmond, and Stratford, one stop east, provides the Central, Jubilee and Elizabeth lines as well as national rail. Journeys to the City and West End therefore usually involve a change, and late-night transport options are limited compared with more central areas; many residents cycle along the canal. Hackney Wick suits young creatives and people who want canal-side living near a huge park, and who accept an evolving, sometimes rough-edged neighbourhood and a commute that begins on the Overground.",
  ],

  // ── Tower Hamlets ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

  "bethnal-green": [
    "Bethnal Green's biggest asset is its tube station. The Central line reaches Liverpool Street in one stop, Bank in two and Oxford Circus in around fifteen minutes, which makes it one of the fastest-connected places in east London for the rent. Cambridge Heath, on the Overground, adds a quick direct run to Liverpool Street.",
    "The area is historically working-class and remains strongly mixed. Bethnal Green Road has a daily street market and a long run of cafés, bakeries and shops, and the streets towards Brick Lane have a large Bangladeshi community. Columbia Road's Sunday flower market is on the western edge, and the Young V&A, a museum of childhood and design, sits beside the station in a Victorian iron-and-glass building. York Hall, a public baths and famous boxing venue, is a local institution.",
    "Victoria Park, one of east London's largest and most beautiful parks, borders the area to the north-east, with lakes, sports fields and cafés, and the Regent's Canal runs along its edge. Closer to the station, Bethnal Green Gardens and Museum Gardens provide smaller green spaces.",
    "The housing is dominated by post-war council estates, alongside Victorian terraces, converted warehouses and new-build developments. That mix creates a varied neighbourhood where a quiet conservation street can sit next to a large estate or a busy main road. Some streets feel lively and safe late into the evening, others less so. Bethnal Green suits people who want a fast tube commute, easy access to Shoreditch and Victoria Park, and a genuinely mixed east London community at rents below Shoreditch next door.",
  ],

  bow: [
    "Bow sits on the eastern edge of inner London, where Tower Hamlets meets the River Lea and the Olympic Park, and it has two quite different sides. North of Bow Road, the streets around Roman Road — especially the conservation areas near Victoria Park — are lined with Victorian terraces, local cafés and shops, and a traditional street market. South of Bow Road, the area is dominated by larger estates, busy roads and industrial land along the river.",
    "Green space is a major attraction. Victoria Park forms the area's north-western boundary, the Olympic Park lies across the Lea to the east, and the canal and river towpaths connect them. Mile End Park and the Tower Hamlets Cemetery Park, a wooded nature reserve, are also close. Three Mills, a historic tidal mill on the Lea, is at the southern edge.",
    "Bow Quarter, a gated development in the former Bryant and May match factory where the famous matchgirls' strike took place in 1888, is one of the area's landmarks, and new-build flats near the river and Bow Church DLR station add to the rental supply. Many homes on the estates are social housing, and the area has a large Bangladeshi community.",
    "Transport is good. Bow Road station has the District and Hammersmith & City lines into the City and beyond, and Bow Church on the DLR runs to Stratford and Canary Wharf. Mile End, a short walk west, adds the Central line. The A12 and the Bow Flyover roundabout are major barriers and sources of traffic noise on the eastern side. Bow suits people who want east London value, parks on several sides and straightforward connections to both the City and Stratford.",
  ],

  whitechapel: [
    "Whitechapel is one of the most intense, historic and diverse neighbourhoods in London. Whitechapel Road's street market runs for much of the week in front of the Royal London Hospital, a vast modern building that dominates the centre and makes the area a major hub for doctors, nurses and medical students. Tower Hamlets council now occupies the hospital's old Victorian building as its town hall.",
    "The area is the heart of Britain's Bangladeshi community, and Brick Lane, Banglatown and the East London Mosque are nearby. Whitechapel Gallery, one of the city's most important contemporary art spaces, stands at the western end near Aldgate East, and Altab Ali Park is a small, symbolic green space beside it. Tourist walking tours retrace the area's Victorian history.",
    "Whitechapel station has been transformed by the Elizabeth line, which reaches Liverpool Street in a couple of minutes, Tottenham Court Road and Bond Street in around ten, and Canary Wharf and Heathrow directly. The District, the Hammersmith & City and the Overground also stop here, the last running north to Shoreditch and Dalston and south to Canada Water. Very few places in London have more options from one entrance.",
    "Housing is a mix of large council estates, Victorian terraces, converted warehouses and new residential towers, especially towards Aldgate. Rents are generally below those in Shoreditch or the City fringe. The trade-offs are constant bustle, crowded pavements, traffic along Whitechapel Road and limited green space beyond small parks; the nearest large ones are Victoria Park and Mile End Park. Whitechapel suits people who work at the hospital, in the City or at Canary Wharf, and who want exceptional transport and a vibrant, multicultural neighbourhood rather than calm.",
  ],

  "mile-end": [
    "Mile End is organised around one of east London's most useful stations. The Central line and the District and Hammersmith & City lines all stop here, and eastbound and westbound interchanges are cross-platform, so switching between the fast Central line to the City and West End and the District towards Tower Hill or Upminster is quick.",
    "Queen Mary University of London has a large campus on Mile End Road, which gives the area a large student population and plenty of cafés and cheap food. The Regent's Canal runs through the campus and continues south towards Limehouse, and Mile End Park, a long linear park, follows it — crossing Mile End Road on the Green Bridge, planted with trees and grass, which carries the park over the traffic below.",
    "Green space is one of Mile End's strengths. Victoria Park is a short walk north along the canal, and Tower Hamlets Cemetery Park, a wild, wooded Victorian cemetery turned nature reserve, is just to the east. The area has sports centres, a climbing wall and an arts pavilion within the park.",
    "The housing includes Georgian terraces around Tredegar Square, Victorian terraces, large council estates, new-build developments and purpose-built student accommodation. Main roads, including Mile End Road and Burdett Road, are busy and noisy, and the area around the station can feel functional rather than attractive. Mile End suits students, young professionals and people working at Canary Wharf, Stratford or in the City who want a three-line station, canal-side parks and lower rents than Bethnal Green or Shoreditch.",
  ],

  wapping: [
    "Wapping is a riverside neighbourhood of cobbled lanes and converted warehouses, and it is one of the quietest places to live this close to the City. Wapping High Street runs along the Thames, lined with tall Victorian warehouses turned into flats, old wharves, the river police station and some of London's oldest riverside pubs.",
    "The area was the heart of London's docks for centuries, and after the docks closed it was redeveloped in the 1980s with a mixture of warehouse conversions, new private housing and small parks. St Katharine Docks, a marina with restaurants and moored boats beside Tower Bridge, is at the western edge, and Tobacco Dock, a restored warehouse complex, is in the centre. The Thames Path offers long riverside walks in both directions.",
    "The housing is dominated by warehouse conversions and newer riverside flats, many with river views and high service charges, alongside council estates further from the water. Local shopping is limited, and the area can feel very quiet, even deserted, on weekday evenings and in winter. That calm is the attraction for many residents, especially professionals working in the City or at Canary Wharf.",
    "Wapping station is on the Overground and sits deep underground, reached by long stairs and lifts; it is one of the oldest stations in the world, built into Brunel's Thames Tunnel. Trains run north to Whitechapel and Shoreditch and south under the river to Canada Water and beyond. Tower Hill and Shadwell, both a walk away, provide the District, Circle and DLR, and cycling to the City takes minutes. Wapping suits people who want a riverside home, quiet streets and quick access to the City, and who do not need nightlife or a busy high street nearby.",
  ],

  // ── Wandsworth ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  tooting: [
    "Tooting has one of the best concentrations of South Asian food in Britain. Upper Tooting Road and Tooting High Street, running either side of Tooting Broadway station, are lined with Tamil, Sri Lankan, Pakistani, Gujarati and Punjabi restaurants, sweet shops, grocers and sari shops, and the area's two indoor markets, Tooting Market and Broadway Market, have added bars, street food and small independent traders to their long-standing stalls.",
    "It is also a hospital town. St George's, one of London's largest teaching hospitals and home to a medical school, is on the southern side of the area, and many doctors, nurses and students rent locally, which gives Tooting a younger, more transient population than its family-house streets suggest.",
    "Tooting Bec Common, on the northern side, is large and varied, with woodland, sports pitches and Tooting Bec Lido, a vast open-air swimming pool opened in 1906 where members swim year-round. Tooting Common and Wandsworth Common are both within reach. The Granada, an extravagantly decorated 1930s cinema that is now a bingo hall, is one of London's most spectacular listed interiors.",
    "The Northern line's Morden branch has two stations here, Tooting Broadway and Tooting Bec, reaching the City in about twenty-five minutes and the West End via Kennington; peak trains are crowded by the time they reach Clapham. Housing is mostly Victorian and Edwardian terraced houses, many converted into flats, along with mansion blocks and the early LCC cottage streets of Totterdown Fields. Rents are below those in Clapham and Balham, though the gap has narrowed. Tooting suits people who value food, green space and a tube line, and who want a lived-in high street rather than a polished one.",
  ],

  battersea: [
    "Battersea is really two neighbourhoods. On the riverside, the restored Battersea Power Station is now a shopping, office and residential complex, surrounded by new towers stretching east through Nine Elms to Vauxhall, with its own station at the end of the Northern line extension. Inland, the older Battersea of Victorian terraces runs south towards Clapham Junction and Northcote Road, a busy street of cafés, shops and restaurants popular with young families.",
    "Battersea Park is the constant between them. It is one of London's best riverside parks, with a boating lake, sports facilities, a children's zoo, sculptures, the Peace Pagoda looking across the Thames towards Chelsea, and wide paths that are full of runners and cyclists every morning. Battersea Dogs and Cats Home, a local institution, is nearby.",
    "The new-build riverside flats come with concierges, gyms, pools and river views, and rents that reflect those amenities; the rental market there is large and professional. The Victorian parts are more varied, with converted flats, family houses, mansion blocks and notable nineteenth-century workers' cottages such as those of the Shaftesbury Park estate, as well as large post-war council estates.",
    "Transport depends on which Battersea you choose. The Northern line from Battersea Power Station and Nine Elms reaches the West End via Kennington in around fifteen minutes; Battersea Park and Queenstown Road stations have trains to Victoria and Waterloo; and Clapham Junction, one of the busiest rail stations in the country, serves the southern half. The riverside can feel corporate and quiet at night, while the streets near Clapham Junction are livelier. Battersea suits both new-build renters who want river and park on the doorstep and families who want period houses near good rail links.",
  ],

  putney: [
    "Putney is defined by the Thames. Its embankment, west of Putney Bridge, is lined with rowing clubs whose boats are carried down to the water every morning, and it is the starting point of the Oxford and Cambridge Boat Race each spring, when the riverbank fills with spectators. Pubs and restaurants along the river make it one of the pleasantest waterfronts in south-west London.",
    "Putney High Street, running uphill from the bridge, is a busy shopping street with chains, cafés and a cinema, and it carries heavy traffic; the side streets are quieter and residential. Putney Heath and Wimbledon Common lie to the south, and Richmond Park, the largest of London's royal parks, is a short bus ride or cycle away through Roehampton.",
    "The housing includes Victorian and Edwardian terraces, many split into flats, large mansion blocks, riverside new builds and, towards Roehampton, large post-war estates. Putney appeals to a slightly older and more settled crowd than Clapham: professionals in their late twenties and thirties, couples and families, and people who row, run or cycle.",
    "There are two main transport options. Putney station has frequent South Western Railway trains to Clapham Junction and Waterloo, taking around fifteen to twenty minutes; East Putney, on the District line's Wimbledon branch, runs across the river towards Earl's Court and central London; and Putney Bridge station, just over the bridge in Fulham, is also on the District line. Journeys to the City and east London are slow and usually need a change. Putney suits people who want river, parks and a sociable but calm neighbourhood, and whose work is in the West End, Westminster or south-west London.",
  ],

  balham: [
    "Balham was once mocked as the Gateway to the South in a famous comedy sketch, and it has turned the joke into a local badge of honour. Today it is a busy, well-served neighbourhood that attracts professionals and young families who find Clapham too expensive or too loud. Balham High Road has supermarkets, gyms, restaurants and bars, and Hildreth Street, a small pedestrianised market street, adds grocers and cafés.",
    "Transport is its main selling point. Balham station has both the Northern line, with direct trains to the City and West End, and Southern rail services to Victoria in around ten minutes, which makes it more flexible than many south London neighbourhoods. The Northern line trains are often crowded by the time they arrive from Tooting.",
    "Balham sits between three commons. Clapham Common is to the north, Wandsworth Common to the west and Tooting Bec Common, with its lido, to the south-east, all within walking distance. That gives the area outstanding access to open space for running, sport and dog walking.",
    "The housing includes large Victorian and Edwardian terraces, especially in the Heaver Estate conservation area, where substantial houses have been divided into flats or kept as family homes, and big mansion blocks such as Du Cane Court, a vast art deco building on the High Road that was once among the largest private apartment blocks in Europe. Rents are generally a step below Clapham but above Tooting. The trade-off is a less distinctive character than its neighbours; Balham is comfortable, convenient and sociable rather than exciting. It suits commuters, couples and young families who want transport, parks and everyday amenities in one place.",
  ],

  earlsfield: [
    "Earlsfield is a quiet residential neighbourhood in the valley of the River Wandle, between Wandsworth Town, Tooting and Wimbledon Park. Its high street, Garratt Lane, is modest: local pubs, cafés, restaurants, gyms and small shops, busy on weekday evenings but rarely crowded, and very much aimed at the people who live there.",
    "The attraction is fast rail with a village pace. Trains from Earlsfield reach Waterloo in about a quarter of an hour, calling at Clapham Junction on the way, which makes central London accessible without the crowding of the Northern line. There is no tube, and journeys to the City or east London usually need a change; buses connect to Tooting Broadway for the Northern line.",
    "Green space surrounds the area. The Wandle Trail follows the river north to the Thames and south towards Merton and Croydon, King George's Park runs alongside the Wandle, and Wandsworth Common, Magdalen Park and Wimbledon Park are all within walking or cycling distance. The streets are generally flat, making cycling straightforward.",
    "Much of the housing is late Victorian and Edwardian terraced houses laid out in regular grids, often called the Magdalen Park estate, with many still whole family houses and others converted into flats. That stock makes Earlsfield popular with young families and couples in their thirties, and the area has a strong community feel and busy primary schools. Nightlife is limited to pubs, and those looking for bars or restaurants in quantity head to Clapham or Wandsworth Town. It is a good fit for commuters to Westminster, the West End or the City and want quick trains, parks and a calm, settled neighbourhood.",
  ],

  // ── Kensington & Chelsea ────────────────────────────────────────────

  "notting-hill": [
    "Notting Hill is one of London's most famous neighbourhoods, and much of what people imagine is really there: pastel-painted terraces, grand stucco crescents and private communal gardens, Portobello Road's antique stalls, cafés and boutiques, and one of the most photogenic streetscapes in the city. The Ladbroke Estate, on the hill west of Portobello Road, is laid out around large shared gardens hidden behind the houses.",
    "The area is also far more mixed than its image. Social housing, particularly in North Kensington towards Ladbroke Grove and Golborne Road, sits close to some of the most expensive homes in Britain, and the Caribbean community that settled here after the Second World War created the Notting Hill Carnival, which takes over the streets every August bank holiday weekend and is one of the largest street festivals in Europe. Trellick Tower, Ernő Goldfinger's brutalist landmark, overlooks the northern end.",
    "Portobello Road Market is busiest on Saturdays, when the antiques section draws large crowds of visitors; on other days the street has fruit and vegetable stalls, food and vintage clothes further north under the Westway flyover. Westbourne Grove and Ledbury Road have upmarket boutiques and restaurants, and the Electric Cinema, one of Britain's oldest working cinemas, is on Portobello Road.",
    "Notting Hill Gate station has the Central, Circle and District lines, giving fast access to the West End and the City, and Ladbroke Grove and Westbourne Park serve the northern parts on the Hammersmith & City and Circle lines. Hyde Park, Kensington Gardens and Holland Park are all a short walk away. Rents are very high, and flats are often small for the price. Notting Hill suits people who want character, markets and central west London living, and who can accept tourists, Carnival weekend and the cost.",
  ],

  kensington: [
    "Kensington is prime west London, built around Kensington High Street and the royal parks. Kensington Palace sits at the western edge of Kensington Gardens, which merges with Hyde Park to form one of the largest green spaces in central London, and Holland Park lies to the north-west of the High Street.",
    "The area has an exceptional cultural offer. The Design Museum occupies the former Commonwealth Institute building on Kensington High Street, and South Kensington, a short walk away, holds the Victoria and Albert Museum, the Natural History Museum, the Science Museum, the Royal Albert Hall and Imperial College. Kensington Church Street is known for antique shops.",
    "The housing includes grand stucco terraces, garden squares, red-brick mansion blocks and quiet cobbled mews, many split into flats. Large numbers of homes are owned by international buyers or used as second homes, and the rental market is dominated by high-end, often furnished flats. Embassies and diplomatic residences are common in the streets around the palace.",
    "High Street Kensington station has the Circle and District lines, with Gloucester Road and South Kensington adding the Piccadilly line towards the West End and Heathrow. Buses along Kensington High Street run east to Knightsbridge and Piccadilly and west to Hammersmith. The neighbourhood is safe, clean and quiet, with good restaurants but restrained nightlife, and it tends to attract families, older professionals, diplomats and international residents rather than young sharers. Kensington suits people who prioritise parks, museums, beautiful streets and central west London access, and for whom a high rent is less important than the setting.",
  ],

  chelsea: [
    "Chelsea is built along the King's Road, which runs from Sloane Square west towards Fulham. The road was the centre of Swinging London in the 1960s and the birthplace of punk fashion in the 1970s; today it is an upmarket shopping street with fashion boutiques, restaurants and cafés, the Saatchi Gallery at its eastern end and the Royal Court Theatre on Sloane Square.",
    "The riverside is quieter and grander. Cheyne Walk and Chelsea Embankment are lined with historic houses facing the Thames, many associated with artists and writers, and the Chelsea Physic Garden, founded in the seventeenth century, sits behind high walls near the river. The Royal Hospital Chelsea, home to the Chelsea Pensioners, hosts the Chelsea Flower Show each May. Battersea Park is directly across Albert Bridge.",
    "Housing includes stucco terraces, garden squares, red-brick mansion blocks, mews houses and, towards World's End, large council estates. The rental market is dominated by expensive flats and houses, many furnished and aimed at international professionals, bankers and families.",
    "Transport is Chelsea's surprising weakness. Sloane Square, on the Circle and District lines, serves the eastern end, and South Kensington is to the north, but much of the King's Road and the western part of Chelsea is a long walk from any tube station, so buses along the King's Road do a lot of work. Stamford Bridge, Chelsea FC's ground, is actually just over the border in Fulham. Chelsea suits people who want a polished, attractive, affluent neighbourhood with shops, restaurants and the river close by, and who are comfortable relying on buses and paying some of the highest rents in London.",
  ],

  "holland-park": [
    "Holland Park takes its name from the park at its centre, which is one of the most beautiful in London. It combines woodland, formal gardens, sports areas and adventure playgrounds with the Kyoto Garden, a Japanese garden with a waterfall and koi pond, peacocks that wander the paths, and the ruins of Holland House, where Opera Holland Park stages a summer season under a canopy.",
    "The surrounding streets are among the grandest residential streets in the capital. Large detached and semi-detached Victorian villas line broad avenues, many divided into generous flats, and some are embassies or ambassadorial residences. Leighton House, the lavishly decorated home and studio of the Victorian painter Frederic Leighton, is now a museum on the southern side.",
    "The neighbourhood is quiet, secure and very expensive. Holland Park Avenue has a small parade of upmarket shops, cafés and restaurants, and Kensington High Street, Notting Hill and Westfield in Shepherd's Bush are all a short walk or bus ride away for everything else. Nightlife is almost non-existent within the area itself.",
    "Holland Park station, on the Central line, gets to Oxford Circus in roughly a quarter of an hour before carrying on to the City; Shepherd's Bush and Notting Hill Gate provide more options. The rental market is small and dominated by large flats and family houses, often let to families, professionals relocating to London and diplomats, with prices to match. Holland Park suits people who want a peaceful, green and elegant neighbourhood within easy reach of both the West End and west London, and who value the park itself above having a lively high street on their doorstep.",
  ],

  // ── Islington ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  islington: [
    "Islington, in the everyday sense, means the area around Angel and Upper Street, one of the longest and busiest restaurant and bar streets in London. Upper Street runs north from Angel towards Highbury Corner, past theatres such as the Almeida, the Business Design Centre, independent shops and hundreds of places to eat and drink, and it stays busy most evenings of the week.",
    "Behind the main roads is some of the finest Georgian and early Victorian housing in inner London. Barnsbury, Canonbury and the streets around Duncan Terrace and Colebrooke Row are arranged around garden squares and terraces, many now expensive flats or family homes, and the Regent's Canal passes through in a tunnel under Angel before emerging among canal-side houses and moorings. Camden Passage, just off Upper Street, has antique shops and a small market, and Chapel Market is a traditional street market near the station.",
    "Islington has little large green space, and among London boroughs it has one of the lowest amounts of park per resident. Highbury Fields, at the northern end of Upper Street, is the main open space, and the canal towpath offers walks towards King's Cross or Victoria Park.",
    "Transport is excellent. Angel station on the Northern line's Bank branch has some of the longest escalators on the Underground and reaches the City in a few minutes, and Highbury & Islington has the Victoria line, the Overground and Great Northern trains to Moorgate. The City is also an easy cycle or walk. Housing ranges from Georgian terraces and mansion blocks to large council estates and new-build flats. Islington suits professionals who want a central, social and walkable neighbourhood, and who are prepared to pay high rents for small flats in return for restaurants, theatres and quick commutes.",
  ],

  holloway: [
    "Holloway runs along the Holloway Road, part of the main A1 route north out of London, and its character is shaped by that busy, noisy road. The stretch around the Nag's Head junction is a large shopping area with chains, discount stores, a market and a wide range of cafés and restaurants, while the university buildings of London Metropolitan University line the southern end.",
    "The area sits between some of north London's most sought-after neighbourhoods — Highbury, Tufnell Park and Islington — and shares their Victorian terraced streets in its quieter parts, especially towards Highbury and Tufnell Park. It also contains large council estates, student housing and newer blocks, among them the redevelopment of the former Holloway Prison site into homes and a park.",
    "Arsenal's Emirates Stadium is a short walk away, and on match days the streets, pubs and stations fill with fans. Green space is limited: the nearest large spaces are Highbury Fields, Caledonian Park and, further north, Hampstead Heath and Parkland Walk.",
    "Holloway Road station, on the Piccadilly line, reaches King's Cross in a few minutes and the West End soon after, and Caledonian Road and Arsenal stations are close by. Highbury & Islington, at the southern end of the Holloway Road, adds the Victoria line and Overground, and Upper Holloway has Overground trains east and west. Rents are generally below those in Islington, Highbury or Tufnell Park, making the area popular with students, young professionals and sharers. Holloway suits people who want central north London transport and lower rents, and who do not mind a busy, unglamorous main road and uneven streets.",
  ],

  archway: [
    "Archway takes its name from the bridge that carries Hornsey Lane over the deep cutting of Archway Road, and it sits at the foot of Highgate Hill where several main roads meet. For years its centre was a hostile traffic gyratory; that has been removed and replaced by Navigator Square, a pedestrianised public space, and the tower above the station has been converted into flats.",
    "The area is a good base for exploring north London's hills and green spaces. Highgate Hill climbs steeply north past the Whittington Hospital, a major local employer, and a statue of Dick Whittington's cat, towards Waterlow Park and Highgate Village. Hampstead Heath is a walk or short bus ride to the west, and the Parkland Walk and the two ancient woods beyond Highgate lie north-east.",
    "Housing includes Victorian terraces and conversions, large council estates, mansion blocks and new-build flats near the station, with quieter conservation areas around Whitehall Park and towards Dartmouth Park. Because rents undercut Highgate, Kentish Town and Tufnell Park, the area draws young professionals, hospital staff, couples and sharers.",
    "Archway station is on the Northern line's High Barnet branch, reaching the City in around fifteen minutes and the West End via Camden; buses run in all directions along Holloway Road, Junction Road and Archway Road. The local high street has cafés, pubs, restaurants and shops, and Junction Road and Tufnell Park nearby add more choice, but evenings are generally low-key. Archway suits people who want a tube line, lower rents and quick access to Highgate and the Heath, and who can tolerate busy roads and a centre that is still gradually improving.",
  ],

  // ── Hammersmith & Fulham ────────────────────────────────────────────

  hammersmith: [
    "Hammersmith is a west London hub first and a neighbourhood second. Its centre, Hammersmith Broadway, is a large bus station and shopping centre wrapped in a gyratory and overshadowed by the A4 flyover, and it is busy, functional and not especially attractive. Two separate tube stations sit on either side of the road: one for the District and Piccadilly lines, the other for the Hammersmith & City and Circle lines.",
    "Walk ten minutes south and Hammersmith becomes something else. The riverside between Hammersmith Bridge and Chiswick, along the Upper and Lower Mall, is lined with rowing clubs, Georgian houses and historic pubs, and it is one of the most attractive stretches of the Thames in London. Ravenscourt Park and Furnivall Gardens provide green space, and the Thames Path runs west towards Chiswick and Kew.",
    "The area has a strong cultural offer for a transport hub. The Eventim Apollo hosts major concerts and comedy, the Lyric Hammersmith is a well-regarded theatre, and Riverside Studios, by the river, combines a cinema, theatre and bar. King Street, running west, is a long, mixed high street with restaurants and shops, and Brackenbury Village, to its north, is a quiet pocket of terraced streets and small shops.",
    "The transport links are the reason many people choose Hammersmith. Four lines give direct routes into the West End, the City, Earl's Court and Paddington, and the Piccadilly line runs straight to Heathrow. Housing ranges from Victorian terraces and mansion blocks to riverside new builds and council estates. Rents are generally below Chelsea and Kensington, though not cheap. Hammersmith suits people who commute across London or travel often, and who are happy to trade a charming centre for connections and a beautiful riverside nearby.",
  ],

  "shepherds-bush": [
    "Shepherd's Bush is organised around a large triangular green, and around it a surprising amount happens. Westfield London, one of the largest shopping centres in Europe, sits on its north-western corner; the Shepherd's Bush Empire, a historic music hall that is now a major concert venue, faces the green; and the Bush Theatre, known for new writing, occupies a converted Victorian library on the Uxbridge Road.",
    "North of Westfield, White City has been transformed. The BBC's former Television Centre has been converted into flats, restaurants, a hotel and studios, and Imperial College has built a large research and innovation campus nearby. Loftus Road, the home of Queens Park Rangers, is also in the area. Shepherd's Bush Market, under the railway viaduct to the west of the green, is a long-running street market with food, fabrics and household goods.",
    "Transport options are numerous. Shepherd's Bush station on the Central line reaches Oxford Circus in around fifteen minutes; Shepherd's Bush Market and Goldhawk Road sit on the elevated Hammersmith & City and Circle route; Wood Lane and White City add more Circle and Central line access; and the rail station at Shepherd's Bush has Overground and Southern services north towards Willesden and south towards Clapham Junction.",
    "The housing mix includes Victorian terraces and conversions, especially around Askew Road and towards Brook Green, mansion blocks, large estates in White City and new-build flats near Westfield and Television Centre. Main roads, especially around the green and Wood Lane, are noisy and congested. Rents are generally lower than in Notting Hill, Holland Park or Kensington nearby. Shepherd's Bush suits people who want quick access to the West End, abundant shopping and entertainment, and a diverse, busy neighbourhood rather than a quiet village.",
  ],

  fulham: [
    "Fulham is a residential district of long, straight streets of Victorian and Edwardian terraced houses stretching from Earl's Court down to the Thames. It has long been popular with young professionals, particularly from finance, law and consultancy, and with families, and its high streets reflect that: gastropubs, gyms, brunch cafés, boutiques and restaurants along Fulham Road, New King's Road and Munster Road.",
    "Two football clubs play here. Stamford Bridge, home of Chelsea FC, is at the eastern edge near Fulham Broadway, and Craven Cottage, Fulham FC's riverside ground, is next to Bishop's Park; match days bring large crowds, busy pubs and heavy traffic. Fulham Palace, the former country residence of the Bishops of London, sits beside Bishop's Park with its walled garden and museum, and the river path runs along the edge.",
    "Parsons Green, a small triangular green surrounded by pubs and shops, is one of Fulham's social hubs, and South Park offers sports and open space. Towards Sands End, the riverside has been redeveloped with new apartment buildings around Imperial Wharf, which has its own Overground station. The Hurlingham Club, a private sports club with large grounds, occupies a stretch of the river.",
    "The District line's Wimbledon branch has three stations — Fulham Broadway, Parsons Green and Putney Bridge — with trains to Earl's Court, Victoria, Westminster and the City, but journeys can be slow and the line is crowded at peak times. There is no Central, Jubilee or Northern line access nearby, and many residents use buses along Fulham Road and King's Road or cycle. Rents are high but generally below Chelsea. Fulham suits sociable professionals and young families who want period houses, pubs and river walks in a polished neighbourhood.",
  ],

  // ── Barnet ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  finchley: [
    "Finchley is a large, suburban part of north London made up of several centres: Church End around Finchley Central station, East Finchley on the Northern line's High Barnet branch, and North Finchley further up the high road, with its arts centre and shops. Each has its own character, but together they feel settled, green and family-oriented.",
    "Finchley Central lies on the High Barnet arm of the Northern line, with a short shuttle branch to Mill Hill East; trains reach Camden in around fifteen minutes and the City and West End in twenty-five to thirty. East Finchley station, with its famous archer statue on the roof, sits closer to Hampstead Garden Suburb and Highgate. Buses run along the main roads, and the North Circular crosses the area, bringing heavy traffic to its junctions.",
    "Green space is abundant. The Dollis Valley Greenwalk follows the Dollis Brook through Finchley, and Avenue House's grounds, Victoria Park and several playing fields provide open space close to most homes. Hampstead Heath and Highgate Wood are within easy reach to the south, and Coldfall Wood is near East Finchley.",
    "Housing consists largely of interwar semi-detached houses, Edwardian and Victorian terraces and mansion blocks, many with gardens. The rental market includes flats in converted houses and purpose-built blocks, as well as family houses, and the area's schools make it popular with families. Finchley has a large Jewish community, and many shops, synagogues and bakeries reflect that. Evenings are quiet, with pubs, restaurants and a cinema but little nightlife. Finchley suits families and people who want more space and calm for their rent, and who can accept a longer tube journey into central London.",
  ],

  hendon: [
    "Hendon is a north-west London suburb shaped by big roads, a university and some notable institutions. Middlesex University has its main campus here, the Metropolitan Police training college is based nearby, and the RAF Museum at Colindale, on the site of the historic Hendon airfield, is one of the largest aviation museums in Britain.",
    "Hendon Central, on the Northern line's Edgware branch, is the main station, with trains to Camden in around fifteen minutes and on to the West End and the City. Hendon station, on Thameslink, has direct trains to St Pancras, Farringdon and Blackfriars. Brent Cross, the long-established shopping centre on the North Circular, is on the southern edge, and a large new district around Brent Cross West station, on Thameslink, is being built next to it.",
    "The main roads dominate much of the area. The A41 Watford Way, the A406 North Circular and the start of the M1 all pass through or close by, so some parts feel car-oriented and noisy. Away from them, streets of interwar semi-detached houses and Edwardian terraces are quiet and residential. Hendon Park, beside Hendon Central, is the main local green space, and the Welsh Harp reservoir, a nature reserve and sailing lake, lies to the west.",
    "The area has large Jewish, South Asian and student communities, with kosher bakeries and restaurants, family-run shops and student-oriented cafés. Housing includes family houses, flats in converted houses, purpose-built blocks and student accommodation. Rents are generally below those in Golders Green, Finchley or West Hampstead. Hendon suits students, families and people seeking value in north-west London with a Northern line or Thameslink connection, and who can live without nightlife or a picturesque high street.",
  ],

  "golders-green": [
    "Golders Green grew up around its Underground station, which opened in 1907 in open fields and triggered rapid suburban development. Today Golders Green Road and Finchley Road meet near the station, with a busy bus station and a coach stop used by long-distance services to airports and cities around Britain.",
    "The area is best known as the centre of one of London's largest Jewish communities. Golders Green Road is lined with kosher bakeries, delis, restaurants, bookshops and grocers, alongside a growing number of Japanese, Korean and other East Asian restaurants and supermarkets. On Friday evenings and Saturdays, parts of the area are noticeably quieter as many shops close for the Sabbath.",
    "Green space is a major advantage. Golders Hill Park, with its small zoo, flower gardens and café, adjoins Hampstead Heath, and the Heath Extension runs along the edge of Hampstead Garden Suburb, the early twentieth-century planned suburb of Arts and Crafts houses, greens and hedged lanes that borders Golders Green to the north-east.",
    "Trains from Golders Green, on the Edgware side of the Northern line, reach Camden in around ten minutes and the West End and City in around twenty-five. Housing is mainly Edwardian and interwar houses, semi-detached and terraced, plus mansion blocks and purpose-built flats near the station and along Finchley Road. Rents are generally lower than in Hampstead or Belsize Park but higher than in Hendon. Evenings are quiet, with restaurants rather than bars. Golders Green suits families, observant Jewish households, and people who want north London greenery and a reliable tube line in a calm, settled neighbourhood.",
  ],

  // ── Richmond upon Thames ────────────────────────────────────────────

  barnes: [
    "Barnes sits inside a loop of the Thames in south-west London, and that geography makes it feel unusually self-contained. Its centre is a genuine village, with a pond and green at its heart, independent shops, cafés, pubs and restaurants along Church Road, and the Olympic Studios, a former recording studio where many famous albums were made, now a cinema and café.",
    "Green space surrounds the area. Barnes Common, a large area of heath, woodland and grassland, lies to the south, and the London Wetland Centre, a nature reserve created on disused reservoirs, attracts birdwatchers and families throughout the year. The Thames Path runs along the riverbank, and the final stretch of the Boat Race passes Barnes each spring on its way to Mortlake.",
    "Housing includes Victorian and Edwardian villas and terraces, riverside houses, mansion blocks, cottages in narrow lanes, and the Castelnau estate, an interwar development of cottage-style homes. Much of the stock is large family houses, so the rental market is small and expensive, and flats in converted houses or small blocks are the most common options.",
    "There is no tube. Barnes and Barnes Bridge stations have South Western Railway services to Waterloo via Putney and Clapham Junction or via Chiswick and Richmond, and buses run to Hammersmith and Putney. Hammersmith Bridge, the most direct route north across the river, has been subject to long closures to vehicle traffic, which has made the area feel more cut off for anyone who relies on buses or driving towards Hammersmith. Barnes suits families and people who want peace, a village atmosphere and access to wildlife and the river, and who can accept higher rents and a rail-based commute.",
  ],

  richmond: [
    "Richmond combines a busy town centre with some of the most beautiful green space in London. Richmond Park, the largest of the royal parks, with herds of red and fallow deer roaming across heath, woodland and ponds, sits at the top of Richmond Hill, and the view from the hill over the Thames valley is the only view in England protected by an Act of Parliament.",
    "The town centre has a good mix of independent and chain shops, cafés, restaurants and pubs, as well as Richmond Theatre, a Victorian building overlooking Richmond Green, a large open space surrounded by historic houses. The riverside, with its terraces, boathouses, rowing boat hire and towpath walks, is busy on sunny weekends. Kew Gardens is a short walk or one stop away, and Petersham Meadows and Ham House are to the south.",
    "Richmond is a terminus for both the District line and the Overground, and mainline trains get to Waterloo in roughly twenty minutes. The District line reaches central London slowly, and the Overground runs across north London to Stratford. Aircraft noise from Heathrow's approach paths affects parts of the area, depending on wind direction.",
    "Housing includes Georgian and Victorian houses, riverside apartments, mansion blocks and interwar family homes. Rents and property prices are among the highest in outer London, and the area is especially popular with families, established professionals and people who want parks, good schools and a strong town centre. Nightlife consists largely of pubs and restaurants, and central London's clubs are a long journey away. Richmond suits people who value green space, the river and a polished, safe town with excellent rail connections, and who are prepared to pay for it.",
  ],

  twickenham: [
    "Twickenham is best known as the home of English rugby. Twickenham Stadium, one of the largest stadiums in Britain, hosts England internationals and major events, and on match days the town fills with tens of thousands of fans, busy pubs, road closures and crowded trains. On other days, Twickenham is a quiet riverside town.",
    "The Thames is its most attractive feature. Church Street, a narrow lane of independent shops and restaurants near the river, leads down to the riverside, where Eel Pie Island, a small island once famous for its 1960s music club, is home to artists' studios and boatyards reached by a footbridge. Marble Hill House and Park, York House Gardens and Radnor Gardens line the river, and Strawberry Hill House, Horace Walpole's eighteenth-century Gothic villa, is a short walk south.",
    "Housing includes Victorian and Edwardian terraces, interwar semi-detached houses, riverside homes and newer flats, generally offering more space for the money than neighbouring Richmond. The area is popular with families, commuters and staff and students at St Mary's University. Local schools and parks make it family-friendly.",
    "Twickenham's trains run via Richmond and Clapham Junction into Waterloo, taking around twenty-five to thirty minutes to Waterloo. There is no tube, and the District line and Overground are reached by changing at Richmond. Journeys to east London, the City and Canary Wharf are long. Heathrow's flight paths affect parts of the area. Twickenham suits families and people who want a riverside town with good schools and value compared with Richmond, and whose work is in central or south-west London rather than the east.",
  ],

  // ── Newham ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  stratford: [
    "Stratford is a regeneration story on a huge scale. The 2012 Olympic Games transformed former industrial land into the Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park, and the area around it now includes Westfield Stratford City, one of Europe's largest shopping centres, the London Stadium, home of West Ham United, the Aquatics Centre and East Bank, a new cultural and education district with museums, performing arts venues and university campuses.",
    "Stratford station is one of the best-connected transport hubs in London. The Central, Jubilee and Elizabeth lines, the DLR, the Overground and national rail services all stop here, and Stratford International, nearby, has high-speed trains to St Pancras and Kent. Journeys to Liverpool Street, Canary Wharf, the West End and the City are quick, and Heathrow is on a direct Elizabeth line route.",
    "Housing is dominated by new-build towers and apartment blocks, many purpose-built for rent. East Village, the former athletes' village, is a large rental neighbourhood with parks, shops and a school, and new developments continue to rise around the station. Older Stratford, to the south and east, has Victorian terraces, council estates and a busy traditional town centre around the Stratford Centre and Theatre Royal Stratford East.",
    "The Olympic Park provides extensive green space, wetlands and riverside paths along the River Lea, connecting north to Hackney Marshes and the Lee Valley. The trade-offs are that much of the neighbourhood feels corporate, centred on large developments and shopping, and that independent culture is still developing. Stratford suits people who want modern flats, exceptional transport, parks and leisure facilities, and who are comfortable with a neighbourhood still being built around them.",
  ],

  "forest-gate": [
    "Forest Gate is a Victorian suburb in north Newham that has become much more accessible since the Elizabeth line began running through its station. Liverpool Street is now about a quarter of an hour away and the West End under half an hour, turning a traditionally overlooked area into a practical commuter base.",
    "Its biggest green asset is Wanstead Flats, a vast expanse of open grassland at the southern tip of Epping Forest, managed by the City of London and used for football, dog walking, running and wildlife. West Ham Park, also managed by the City of London, is to the south-west, and Wanstead Park, with its lakes and woodland, is further north.",
    "Forest Gate's population is exceptionally mixed, with large South Asian, African, Caribbean and Eastern European communities, and Woodgrange Road, the main street near the station, has an eclectic mix of grocers, restaurants, cafés, and a growing number of independent businesses. The Woodgrange Estate, a conservation area of late Victorian villas with decorative porches, is one of the area's best-preserved streetscapes.",
    "Housing is dominated by Victorian terraced houses, many converted into flats, alongside some estates and new developments. Rents are generally lower than in Stratford or Leytonstone, and more space is available for the money. Wanstead Park and Woodgrange Park stations add Overground services across north London. Evenings are quiet, with limited nightlife beyond pubs and restaurants, and residents often head to Stratford or Hackney for more. Forest Gate suits people who want an Elizabeth line commute, open space and value, and who are comfortable in an evolving, multicultural neighbourhood.",
  ],

  // ── Waltham Forest ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

  walthamstow: [
    "Walthamstow's centre is its market. Walthamstow High Street hosts one of the longest outdoor street markets in Europe, around a kilometre of stalls selling fruit, vegetables, fabrics, clothes and household goods, alongside shops and cafés reflecting the area's large South Asian, Eastern European, African and Caribbean communities.",
    "The area has an unusually strong cultural identity for outer London. The William Morris Gallery, in the house where the designer grew up, sits in Lloyd Park, and Walthamstow Village, around Orford Road, is a conservation area of old cottages, independent restaurants and cafés, and the Vestry House Museum. The borough's award-winning Mini Holland scheme has transformed many streets into low-traffic, cycle-friendly routes, and the old greyhound stadium site has become housing.",
    "Walthamstow Central is the northern terminus of the Victoria line, which means you can often get a seat, and trains reach King's Cross in around fifteen minutes and Oxford Circus in around twenty-five. The Overground from Walthamstow Central runs to Liverpool Street, and Blackhorse Road and Walthamstow Queen's Road add Overground services across north London.",
    "Walthamstow Wetlands, one of Europe's largest urban wetland nature reserves, lies to the west, and Epping Forest and the Lee Valley are both within reach. Housing is dominated by Edwardian terraces and the distinctive Warner flats, pairs of upper and lower flats with separate front doors, with some newer developments near Blackhorse Road. Rents remain lower than inner east London, though they have risen fast. Walthamstow suits families and professionals who want green space, a Victoria line connection, strong community life and more space for their money, and who do not need central-London nightlife on their doorstep.",
  ],

  leyton: [
    "Leyton sits between Stratford and Walthamstow, and it combines Central line access with some of east London's best open spaces. From Leyton station it is one stop to Stratford and roughly a quarter of an hour to Liverpool Street, and the Central line continues directly to the West End, making commuting straightforward if you live near the station.",
    "The area borders the Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park and the Lee Valley to the west. Hackney Marshes, Leyton Marsh and Walthamstow Marshes provide large expanses of grassland, riverside paths and sports pitches, and the Lee Valley Ice Centre and Riding Centre are both nearby. Leyton Jubilee Park and Coronation Gardens add smaller green spaces within the neighbourhood.",
    "Leyton Orient, one of London's oldest football clubs, plays at Brisbane Road, and match days bring crowds to the local pubs. Francis Road, a short street of independent cafés, bakeries, restaurants and shops, has become the area's social hub, and Leyton High Road offers a wider mix of shops and takeaways. The High Road carries heavy traffic and can feel rundown in parts.",
    "Most homes are late Victorian terraces, plenty of them split into flats, with estates and new-build blocks near the station and the Olympic Park. Rents are generally lower than in Hackney, Walthamstow and Stratford. Leyton Midland Road station, on the Overground, adds routes west across north London and east to Barking. Evenings are generally quiet, with a few good pubs and restaurants but little nightlife. Leyton suits young professionals, couples and families who want value, access to large parks and a Central line connection, and who choose their street based on its distance from the station.",
  ],

  // ── Ealing ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  ealing: [
    "Ealing has long been called the Queen of the Suburbs, and its broad, tree-lined streets, large Victorian and Edwardian houses and generous parks still justify the nickname. It is also a substantial town in its own right, with a busy shopping centre around Ealing Broadway, restaurants and pubs, a theatre scene, and Ealing Studios, the oldest continuously working film studio in the world.",
    "The Elizabeth line changed Ealing's relationship with central London. Ealing Broadway station now has Elizabeth line trains to Paddington, Bond Street, Tottenham Court Road and the City, alongside the Central and District lines and Great Western Railway services, and Heathrow is a short direct ride away. Journeys that once took forty minutes on the tube are now far quicker.",
    "Green space is one of Ealing's main strengths. Walpole Park, beside Pitzhanger Manor, the country house designed by Sir John Soane, hosts summer festivals; Ealing Common and Haven Green are close to the centre; and Lammas Park and Pitshanger Park are within walking distance. Brentham Garden Suburb, a planned early twentieth-century estate of Arts and Crafts houses, is to the north.",
    "Housing ranges from large family houses and mansion flats to converted Victorian houses, interwar semis and new apartment blocks around the station. Ealing is especially popular with families and professionals, and its schools are a strong draw; there is also a long-established Polish community, reflected in shops, churches and cafés. After dark the town settles into pubs and restaurants; anyone after clubs heads into central London. Ealing suits people who want a green, safe, well-served suburb with exceptional transport links, and who are prepared to trade central buzz for space and calm.",
  ],

  acton: [
    "Acton is often said to have more stations bearing its name than anywhere else in Britain, and that abundance defines the area. Acton Town has the District and Piccadilly lines, North and East Acton are on the Central line, Acton Central and South Acton are on the Overground, and Acton Main Line has the Elizabeth line to Paddington, Bond Street and the City. Few suburbs offer so many options.",
    "The area is less polished than neighbouring Chiswick or Ealing, and its high street is practical rather than charming, with supermarkets, takeaways and cafés. It has a long-established Polish community and a significant Japanese presence, including the Japanese School in London. To the north, Park Royal and Old Oak Common form one of London's largest industrial areas and the site of a major regeneration scheme around the new high-speed rail interchange.",
    "Acton has a good share of green space. Acton Park, Gunnersbury Park, with its museum and sports facilities, and Acton Green Common are within easy reach, and Bedford Park, a famous early garden suburb of red-brick houses, lies on the Acton-Chiswick border.",
    "Housing includes Victorian and Edwardian terraces, particularly in the grid of streets known as the Poets' Corner, interwar semi-detached houses, large estates such as South Acton, which has been undergoing extensive regeneration, and new apartment blocks. Rents are generally below Ealing, Chiswick and Shepherd's Bush. The area is quiet in the evenings, with pubs and restaurants but limited nightlife. Acton suits people who want west London value, excellent transport across several lines and access to parks, and who do not mind a less picturesque town centre.",
  ],

  // ── Brent ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  kilburn: [
    "Kilburn High Road follows the line of the Roman road Watling Street, and it forms the boundary between two boroughs, Brent to the west and Camden to the east. It is a long, busy, sometimes chaotic high street of supermarkets, discount shops, pubs, cafés and restaurants, reflecting the area's long history as a centre of London's Irish community and its more recent Caribbean, African, Middle Eastern and Eastern European populations.",
    "The Kiln Theatre, formerly the Tricycle, is a well-regarded theatre and cinema on the High Road, and many of the area's pubs host live music. To the west, Queen's Park, a Victorian park managed by the City of London, has a café, sports facilities and a small farm, and the surrounding streets of Queen's Park are among the most sought-after in north-west London.",
    "Transport is excellent. Kilburn station on the Jubilee line reaches Bond Street and Westminster in around fifteen minutes and Canary Wharf directly; Kilburn High Road station has Overground trains to Euston; Brondesbury, on the Overground, runs east to Stratford and west to Richmond; and Kilburn Park, on the Bakerloo line, is at the southern end.",
    "Housing includes Victorian terraces and mansion blocks, many converted into flats, alongside large estates, particularly in South Kilburn, which has been undergoing regeneration. Rents are generally lower than in neighbouring West Hampstead, Queen's Park or Maida Vale, making the area popular with young professionals and sharers. The High Road is noisy, crowded and uneven, and the quality of streets varies widely. Kilburn suits people who want strong transport links, lively pubs and diverse food at more affordable rents, and who are comfortable in a busy, gritty inner north-west London neighbourhood.",
  ],

  cricklewood: [
    "Cricklewood grew up along the Edgware Road, the old Roman Watling Street, and it still feels like a place people pass through on the way elsewhere. Cricklewood Broadway is a long, busy stretch of shops, takeaways, pubs and supermarkets, with a large bus garage and heavy traffic. The area was once a centre of aircraft manufacturing, with Handley Page's factory and airfield, and later of Irish working-class life.",
    "Its main station, Cricklewood, is on Thameslink, with direct trains to St Pancras in around fifteen minutes and on to Farringdon, Blackfriars and London Bridge, and services north to Luton and Bedford. There is no tube, but Willesden Green and Kilburn on the Jubilee line are a bus ride or twenty-minute walk away, and Brent Cross West station, on Thameslink, has opened to the north.",
    "Gladstone Park, on a hill to the west, is the area's main green space, with views over north-west London, a walled garden and sports facilities. The large Brent Cross Town development is transforming the land between Cricklewood and Brent Cross with new homes, offices and parks, and it will change the character of the northern side of the area over the coming years.",
    "The streets are mostly Edwardian and late Victorian terraces, a good share now split into flats, plus some interwar homes and new-build blocks. Rents are generally lower than in West Hampstead, Kilburn or Willesden Green, making it a good-value option in north-west London. Local amenities are practical, with limited nightlife beyond pubs. Cricklewood suits people who work near a Thameslink station, want value close to fashionable parts of north-west London, and are comfortable with a scruffy, busy main road and the gradual pace of regeneration.",
  ],

  // ── Outer boroughs ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

  greenwich: [
    "Greenwich is one of London's most historic and beautiful places to live, and much of its centre forms a UNESCO World Heritage Site. The Old Royal Naval College, designed by Christopher Wren, stands on the riverfront, and behind it the Queen's House, the National Maritime Museum and the Royal Observatory, home of the Prime Meridian, rise through Greenwich Park to the top of the hill, with one of the finest views of London across to Canary Wharf.",
    "The Cutty Sark, a restored nineteenth-century tea clipper, sits beside the river, and Greenwich Market, a covered market of food stalls, crafts and antiques, is busy throughout the week. The Greenwich Foot Tunnel runs under the Thames to the Isle of Dogs, making Canary Wharf a pleasant walk or cycle away. Tourists crowd the centre on weekends and in summer, but residential streets a few minutes away are quiet.",
    "Transport comes in several forms. Cutty Sark and Greenwich stations are on the DLR, with direct trains to Canary Wharf and Bank; the mainline platforms at Greenwich get to London Bridge in roughly ten minutes; and river boats run from Greenwich Pier to central London. The Jubilee line at North Greenwich, near the O2, is a bus ride away. Journeys to the West End and west London are relatively slow.",
    "Housing includes Georgian and Victorian terraces around Crooms Hill and West Greenwich, riverside new builds, mansion blocks and council estates. Rents are generally lower than in comparable parts of central or west London but higher than in neighbouring Deptford. Greenwich suits people who work at Canary Wharf or London Bridge and want parks, history, the river and a village-like atmosphere.",
  ],

  wimbledon: [
    "Wimbledon is known around the world for its tennis championships, and for two weeks each summer the area fills with crowds, queues in Wimbledon Park and busy trains. For the other fifty weeks it is a prosperous, green, family-oriented part of south-west London made up of two quite different halves: the busy town centre around the station and, up the hill, Wimbledon Village.",
    "The Village has boutiques, cafés, restaurants, pubs and riding stables, and it opens onto Wimbledon Common, which together with Putney Heath forms one of the largest areas of common land in London. The common has woodland, heath, ponds and a historic windmill, and it is used for running, horse riding and walking. Cannizaro Park and Wimbledon Park, with its lake, add more green space.",
    "The town centre, down the hill, has a shopping centre, a theatre, cinemas, bars and restaurants, and AFC Wimbledon's stadium at Plough Lane. Wimbledon station is a major interchange: South Western Railway trains reach Waterloo in around fifteen to twenty minutes, the District line runs to Earl's Court and central London, Thameslink trains loop through the south London suburbs to Blackfriars and St Pancras, and trams run east to Croydon.",
    "Housing includes Victorian and Edwardian terraces near the town centre, large detached and semi-detached houses around the Village and the Common, mansion blocks and new apartment buildings near the station. Rents are high, particularly in the Village. Evenings in the Village are quiet; the town centre is livelier. It is a natural choice for households after open space, good schools, safe streets and fast rail links to Waterloo, and who are happy with a longer journey to east London.",
  ],

  "enfield-town": [
    "Enfield Town is a market town at London's northern edge that still feels like one. Its Market Place has held a market under a charter dating back to the early fourteenth century, and around it are a church, historic buildings, pubs, restaurants and a modern shopping centre. The New River, an artificial waterway built in the seventeenth century to bring water to London, winds through the town. Enfield also has an unexpected claim to fame: the world's first cash machine was installed here in 1967.",
    "The area borders the Green Belt, and its green spaces are extensive. Forty Hall, a seventeenth-century manor house, sits in a large estate with farmland, woodland and a lake, and Trent Park, a vast country park with woods, fields and walking trails, lies to the west. Town Park and Chase Green are close to the centre.",
    "Enfield Town station is the terminus of an Overground line to Liverpool Street, with journeys taking around thirty-five to forty minutes, and Enfield Chase station has Great Northern trains to Moorgate. The Piccadilly line at Oakwood is a bus ride away. Journeys into central London are long compared with inner areas, and many residents drive or use buses for local trips.",
    "Housing is mainly interwar semi-detached houses, Victorian and Edwardian terraces, larger detached homes and some modern flats, often with gardens and more space for the money than in inner London. The area is popular with families, and schools are an important draw. Nightlife consists largely of pubs and restaurants. Enfield Town suits families and people who want a traditional town centre, green space and value, and who accept a longer commute to central London.",
  ],

  harrow: [
    "Harrow has two faces. On top of the hill, Harrow on the Hill is a historic village of old buildings, narrow streets and the famous Harrow School, with St Mary's Church crowning the summit and long views across west London. Below it, Harrow town centre is a large, busy shopping district built around two shopping centres, a bus station and Harrow-on-the-Hill station.",
    "That station is the area's main asset. The Metropolitan line runs fast trains to Baker Street in around twenty minutes, stopping at few stations on the way, and Chiltern Railways services run to Marylebone in a similar time. The Bakerloo line and Overground are available at Harrow & Wealdstone, a short bus ride north. For a zone this far out, central London is surprisingly accessible.",
    "Harrow is one of the most diverse boroughs in the country, with a large Gujarati and wider South Asian community, alongside Sri Lankan Tamil, Eastern European, Irish and many others, which is reflected in the temples, churches, mosques, grocers and restaurants along Station Road and beyond. Green space includes Harrow Recreation Ground, West Harrow Recreation Ground and, further north, Harrow Weald Common and Stanmore Common.",
    "Housing is dominated by the interwar semi-detached and terraced houses of Metro-land, the suburbs that grew along the Metropolitan line, alongside flats near the station and some larger homes on the hill. Rents are generally lower than in Ealing or inner west London, with more space and gardens. The town centre is functional rather than charming, and nightlife is limited. Harrow suits families and people who want space, schools and a fast Metropolitan line commute at outer-London rents.",
  ],

  aldgate: [
    "Aldgate is the eastern gateway to the City of London, where the towers of the financial district meet the older streets of the East End. It takes its name from one of the gates in the old London Wall, and today it is a mix of office buildings, hotels, new residential towers, churches and a handful of historic streets. St Botolph's Aldgate, an eighteenth-century church, sits beside Aldgate Square, a public space created when an old traffic gyratory was removed.",
    "Some of London's best-known markets are within minutes. Petticoat Lane Market runs along Middlesex Street, busiest on Sunday mornings; Spitalfields Market, in its Victorian market hall, is a short walk north; and Brick Lane's food, vintage shops and weekend markets are just to the east. The Whitechapel Gallery is at Aldgate East.",
    "Transport is outstanding. Aldgate station has the Circle and Metropolitan lines, Aldgate East serves the District and Hammersmith & City, Tower Hill is a short walk south, and Liverpool Street, with the Elizabeth and Central lines, and Fenchurch Street are within easy walking distance. The City's offices can be reached on foot in minutes, and Canary Wharf is a short DLR ride away from Tower Gateway.",
    "Housing is mainly new-build apartments in residential towers such as Goodman's Fields and Aldgate Place, along with the City of London's own Middlesex Street estate and some converted buildings. Rents are high, although often below Shoreditch or the heart of the City. The area is busy on weekdays, crowded with office workers, and can be quiet on weekends away from the markets. Aldgate suits people who work in the City and want to walk to work, with the East End's food and nightlife on their doorstep.",
  ],

  barking: [
    "Barking is an old town on the River Roding in east London, and one of its most important historic sites is Barking Abbey, founded in the seventh century; its ruins sit beside St Margaret's Church, where Captain James Cook was married. Today the town centre is a busy, practical shopping area with a market, supermarkets, chain stores and a modern town square.",
    "Barking station is a significant transport interchange. The District line and Hammersmith & City line run into central London, c2c services get to Fenchurch Street in about a quarter of an hour, and the Overground runs north-west to Gospel Oak and south to Barking Riverside, a large new development by the Thames with its own station and pier. That combination makes Barking one of the best-connected places in outer east London.",
    "Green space includes Barking Park, with a boating lake and splash park, and Mayesbrook Park and the riverside paths at Barking Riverside. The wider borough contains the Becontree estate, built between the wars as one of the largest public housing estates in the world, and the area has a long working-class history.",
    "Housing ranges from Victorian terraced houses and interwar council houses to new-build apartment blocks near the station and at Barking Riverside, and rents are among the lowest in London. The population is highly diverse, with large African, South Asian and Eastern European communities. Evenings are generally quiet, with limited nightlife and a town centre that can feel rough in places. Barking is for renters who put very low rents, quick trains to the City and extra space first, and who are comfortable with an outer-London, functional town.",
  ],

  ilford: [
    "Ilford is a large, busy town centre in outer east London, and its fortunes changed with the arrival of the Elizabeth line. From Ilford the Elizabeth line now runs straight to Liverpool Street in roughly fifteen minutes, reaches the West End in half an hour and carries on to Heathrow, which makes a formerly overlooked commuter town far more practical for central London jobs.",
    "The town has a large South Asian community, and Ilford Lane, running south from the centre, is lined with restaurants, sweet shops, jewellery and sari shops and grocers, while the main shopping area has a large indoor shopping centre and chain stores. Gants Hill, to the north, has a Central line station with a vaulted concourse inspired by the Moscow Metro.",
    "Valentines Park, one of east London's finest parks, sits a short walk north of the centre, with lakes, formal gardens, sports facilities and Valentines Mansion, a restored seventeenth-century house. The River Roding runs to the west, and Seven Kings Park and Goodmayes Park lie to the east.",
    "Homes range from Victorian and Edwardian terraces to interwar semis and a crop of new apartment towers by the station. Rents undercut Stratford and Forest Gate, and a budget buys noticeably more room. The town centre is busy, congested and sometimes chaotic, and green space immediately around the station is limited. Nightlife is modest, centred on restaurants rather than bars. Ilford works for people who want the Elizabeth line, excellent South Asian food, value and family-sized homes, and who are comfortable with a busy, workaday town.",
  ],

  romford: [
    "Romford is a traditional Essex market town absorbed into London, and it still has one of the region's biggest markets, held in the Market Place under a charter dating back to the thirteenth century. Around it, the town centre is a large shopping destination, with several shopping centres, including The Brewery, built on the site of the old Romford Brewery, and a wide range of chain stores and restaurants.",
    "Unusually for outer London, Romford has a genuine night-time economy. South Street and the surrounding roads are lined with bars, clubs and pubs that draw crowds from across east London and Essex, particularly at weekends, which can make the centre busy and noisy late at night.",
    "Romford station has Elizabeth line trains to Liverpool Street, Tottenham Court Road and Bond Street, taking around thirty minutes to Liverpool Street, and faster Greater Anglia services reach Liverpool Street in around twenty minutes. The Overground runs south to Upminster. Buses serve the wider suburbs, and many residents drive; the town is ringed by a busy road.",
    "Havering is one of London's greenest boroughs. Raphael Park and Lodge Farm Park are close to the centre, and larger country parks, including Bedfords Park and Havering Country Park, lie to the north. Housing is mainly interwar semi-detached houses, Victorian terraces, bungalows and modern flats close to the centre, often with gardens, and rents sit near the bottom of the London range. Romford suits families and people who want space, value, a large town centre and a real nightlife scene, and who are comfortable with a long commute to central or west London.",
  ],

  "crystal-palace": [
    "Crystal Palace is named after the vast glass exhibition hall that was moved here from Hyde Park after the Great Exhibition of 1851 and destroyed by fire in 1936. Its terraces, grand steps and ruined sphinxes survive in Crystal Palace Park, alongside the famous Victorian dinosaur sculptures, a maze, a boating lake, the National Sports Centre and a concert bowl. The television transmitter mast on the hilltop is visible across London.",
    "The neighbourhood sits on one of the highest points in the capital, where five boroughs meet, and its centre, known as the Triangle, is formed by Westow Hill, Westow Street and Church Road. The Triangle has independent shops, antique and vintage stores, cafés, restaurants, pubs and bars, and a strong local identity that residents are protective of. The views from streets on the ridge extend across central London and to the North Downs.",
    "Crystal Palace station has Overground trains north through Forest Hill, Canada Water and Shoreditch to Highbury & Islington, and Southern services to Victoria and London Bridge. Gipsy Hill and Sydenham Hill stations nearby add more rail options. Journeys into central London typically take thirty to forty minutes, and the steep hills make walking and cycling challenging. Crystal Palace FC plays at Selhurst Park, a few miles away, not in the neighbourhood itself.",
    "Housing includes large Victorian villas, many converted into flats, mansion blocks, Victorian terraces and some post-war estates. Rents are generally lower than in Dulwich or Clapham, with more space. Crystal Palace suits people who want a creative, characterful neighbourhood with a large park, independent businesses and views, and who are willing to accept a longer rail-based commute.",
  ],

  bexleyheath: [
    "Bexleyheath is an outer south-east London town centred on a long pedestrianised Broadway, with a shopping centre, supermarkets, chain stores, restaurants and a cinema. It developed rapidly in the twentieth century as a commuter suburb on the edge of Kent, and it retains a strongly suburban character.",
    "It has some notable heritage. Red House, commissioned by William Morris and designed by Philip Webb, is one of the most important houses of the Arts and Crafts movement and is now run by the National Trust. Danson Park, with its lake and restored Georgian villa, is one of the largest parks in the borough, and Hall Place, a Tudor house with extensive gardens, is nearby in Bexley.",
    "Transport is rail-based. Trains from Bexleyheath run to the three City and West End termini of Southeastern in thirty to forty minutes, and there is no tube or DLR. The Elizabeth line at Abbey Wood is a short bus ride to the north, with the Elizabeth line running straight through central London. Many residents rely on cars, and the A2 and A207 are major routes.",
    "Housing is dominated by interwar semi-detached houses, bungalows, terraces and some modern flats near the Broadway, typically with gardens and generous space. Rents are among the lowest in south-east London, and the area is particularly popular with families. Schools, including grammar schools in the borough, are a significant draw. Nightlife is limited to pubs and restaurants. Bexleyheath suits families and people who want space, gardens and value, and who are comfortable with a suburban lifestyle and a longer commute into central London.",
  ],

  croydon: [
    "Croydon is one of the largest town centres in London, with its own skyline of office towers, many built in the 1960s and increasingly converted into flats or replaced with new residential towers. It is a place in the middle of long-running change: its main shopping centre has been awaiting redevelopment for years, while new cultural and food venues have opened around it.",
    "Transport is its greatest strength. East Croydon station has fast Thameslink and Southern trains to London Bridge and Victoria in around fifteen minutes, direct services to Gatwick and Brighton, and trains through the City to St Pancras. West Croydon adds the Overground north to Shoreditch and Highbury, and Croydon's tram network runs east to Beckenham and New Addington and west to Wimbledon.",
    "The town centre has Surrey Street Market, one of the oldest street markets in London, the refurbished Fairfield Halls arts centre, Boxpark food and drink hall by East Croydon station, and a large shopping area. Croydon Minster and the remains of the Archbishop of Canterbury's former palace reflect the town's long history. Green spaces include Wandle Park, Lloyd Park and Addington Hills, where a viewing platform looks towards central London.",
    "Housing ranges from new apartment towers and office conversions near the stations to Victorian terraces in Addiscombe and South Croydon, and interwar suburbs further out. Rents are among the lowest for such fast rail links. The town centre can feel rough and uneven in places, and there are sharp contrasts between regenerated and neglected streets. Croydon suits commuters who want fast trains to London Bridge or Victoria, modern flats and value, and people who travel regularly to Gatwick or the south coast.",
  ],

  kingston: [
    "Kingston upon Thames is an ancient riverside market town where Saxon kings were crowned; the Coronation Stone, said to have been used in those ceremonies, still stands outside the Guildhall. Its historic Market Place, with food stalls and old buildings, sits at the heart of a large shopping centre that draws people from across south-west London and Surrey.",
    "The Thames is Kingston's most attractive feature. Riverside restaurants and bars line the waterfront, the Thames Path runs south towards Hampton Court Palace and its parks, and Bushy Park, with its deer and Diana Fountain, lies across the river. Richmond Park's Kingston Gate is a short walk north-east.",
    "Kingston University and its students give the town a younger feel than many outer suburbs, and there are pubs, bars and a busy evening scene around the riverside and town centre. New Malden, a short bus ride east, has one of the largest Korean communities in Europe.",
    "There is no tube. Kingston station has South Western Railway trains to Waterloo via Wimbledon or Richmond, typically taking around thirty to forty minutes, while Surbiton station, just south of the town centre, has faster trains to Waterloo in around twenty minutes. Homes are a mix of period terraces, interwar semis, riverside flats and purpose-built student blocks. Rents are high for outer London but lower than Richmond, and the choice of flats is wide because of the student and commuter demand. Kingston suits families, students and people who want a lively riverside town with extensive shopping and parks, and whose work allows a longer rail commute into central London.",
  ],

  sutton: [
    "Sutton is a large outer south London town centre built along a long pedestrianised High Street, lined with shops, restaurants and pubs, running uphill from the station. It is a practical, settled suburb with good schools, including several grammar schools, that attract families from across south London and Surrey.",
    "Green spaces are plentiful. Nonsuch Park, on the site of Henry VIII's lost Nonsuch Palace, stretches towards Cheam and Ewell, and Carshalton, next door, has ponds, historic parkland and the Honeywood Museum. Sutton also has BedZED, a pioneering eco-village of energy-efficient homes in Hackbridge, and the Royal Marsden Hospital and Institute of Cancer Research at Belmont, which are major local employers.",
    "Transport is rail-based. Sutton station has Southern trains to London Victoria and London Bridge and Thameslink services through Wimbledon and the City to St Pancras, typically taking around thirty to forty minutes to central London. There is no tube, and journeys to east or north London usually involve changes. Buses connect to Croydon, Kingston and Morden, at the southern end of the Northern line.",
    "Housing is dominated by interwar semi-detached houses and Victorian and Edwardian cottages and terraces, with flats near the town centre and some larger detached homes in Cheam and Belmont. Rents are relatively low for south London and the housing offers more space and gardens. Evenings are quiet, with pubs and restaurants but limited nightlife. Sutton suits families and people who prioritise schools, space, calm residential streets and value, and who can accept a longer commute into central London.",
  ],

  chiswick: [
    "Chiswick is one of west London's most affluent and attractive neighbourhoods, built along Chiswick High Road, a long street of independent shops, cafés, restaurants, pubs and delis. It feels settled and family-oriented, with good schools, well-kept streets and a strong community atmosphere.",
    "Its history and green spaces are highlights. Chiswick House, an eighteenth-century Palladian villa, sits in large landscaped gardens, and Hogarth's House, the country home of the painter William Hogarth, is nearby. Along the Thames, Chiswick Mall and Strand-on-the-Green are lined with elegant houses and riverside pubs, and Bedford Park, one of the earliest garden suburbs, has distinctive red-brick houses by Richard Norman Shaw. Gunnersbury Park, Turnham Green and Chiswick Common add green space.",
    "Turnham Green and Chiswick Park stations are on the District line, which runs to Hammersmith, Earl's Court and central London; the Piccadilly line passes through Turnham Green but stops there only early in the morning and late at night. Gunnersbury has the District line and Overground, and Chiswick station, on the Hounslow loop, runs to Waterloo. Getting to the City or anywhere east takes a while. The Hogarth Roundabout and the elevated M4 and A4 carry heavy traffic, and aircraft approaching Heathrow pass overhead.",
    "Housing includes Victorian and Edwardian terraces and semis, riverside houses, mansion blocks and newer apartments near Chiswick Business Park. Rents are high but generally below Kensington or Chelsea. Chiswick suits families and professionals who want a village-like west London neighbourhood with the river, parks and independent shops close by, and whose work is in west London, Hammersmith or the West End.",
  ],

  uxbridge: [
    "Uxbridge is a historic market town on London's far western edge, and it feels more like a town in the Home Counties than part of the capital. Its centre has a pedestrianised High Street, two shopping centres, restaurants, pubs and a cinema, and the art deco Uxbridge station, designed by Charles Holden, is the terminus of both the Metropolitan and Piccadilly lines.",
    "Brunel University's large campus sits on the southern side of the town, and its students and staff give Uxbridge a younger population and a busier evening scene than many outer suburbs. The Battle of Britain Bunker, the underground operations room from which fighter squadrons were controlled in 1940, is now a museum on the site of the former RAF Uxbridge.",
    "Green space and countryside are close. The Grand Union Canal runs along the town's western edge, and the Colne Valley Regional Park, with lakes, woodland and walking routes, stretches west and north towards the Chiltern Hills. Hillingdon Court Park and Fassnidge Park are within the town.",
    "Journeys into central London are long: the Metropolitan line takes around forty-five minutes to Baker Street, and the Piccadilly line takes longer to the West End. Heathrow is a short bus ride away, and the M40 and M25 are close, so many residents drive. Housing includes interwar semi-detached houses, modern flats near the centre, family homes in the surrounding suburbs and student accommodation, and rents are among the lowest in west London. Uxbridge suits students, families and people who work locally, at Heathrow or in the Thames Valley, and who value space and countryside over proximity to central London.",
  ],

  // ── end of profiles ──
};
